{"questions":[{"_i":195,"q":"25-year-old young man: pleuritic precordial pain, worse when lying down, better when leaning forward. ECG: diffuse concave ST elevation, PR depression. Pericarditis or ACS?","a":["ACS: focal ST elevation in two leads, troponin strongly elevated","Pericarditis: diffuse concave ST elevation + PR depression + posture-dependent pain","Myocarditis: diffuse + young patient","Aortic dissection: tearing pain to the back"],"ex":"Pericarditis: diffuse concave ST elevation, PR depression (pathognomonic), pain better when leaning forward/worse when lying down, often after viral infection. ACS: focal ST elevation (two contiguous leads), no PR depression, troponin elevated, no posture effect on pain.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Pericarditis vs ACS: the ECG distinction is crucial. Pericarditis: diffuse concave ("saddle-shape") ST elevation in all leads + PR depression (most specific sign). ACS: focal ST elevation (regional distribution, contiguous leads) + reciprocal depression.","wiki_redflag":"Tamponade as complication: if pericarditis fluid accumulates — Beck's triad (hypotension + distended neck veins + muffled heart sounds). Pericardiocentesis required. Constrictive pericarditis = late complication (Kussmaul sign, pericardial calcification on echo).","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"PR depression: pathognomonic","desc":"Inflammation of the pericardium also affects the atrial wall → abnormal atrial repolarization → PR depression (seen almost only in pericarditis). Better visible in II and aVF."},{"title":"Diffuse ST elevation","desc":"Unlike ACS (regional distribution): pericardial inflammation is diffuse → ST elevation in almost all leads except aVR (ST depression in aVR). Concave (saddle-shaped) vs convex (dome-shaped) in STEMI."},{"title":"Posture-dependent pain","desc":"Inflammation makes the pericardium rough → friction rub. Leaning forward: the heart moves away from the pericardium → less friction → less pain. Lying flat: more pericardial pressure → more pain."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Pericarditis","desc":"Diffuse concave ST elevation + PR depression + posture-dependent pain (better leaning forward) + pericardial friction rub. Troponin may be mildly elevated (myopericarditis)."},{"label":"STEMI","desc":"Focal ST elevation (regional leads) + reciprocal depressions + troponin strongly elevated. Pain pressing, radiation to arm, not posture-dependent."},{"label":"Myocarditis","desc":"Diffuse + young patient + post-viral. Troponin significantly elevated. ST elevations possible but more irregular pattern. Cardiac MRI = gold standard."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Ibuprofen 600 mg tid + colchicine 0.5 mg bid — 3 months for recurrence prevention.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Rest","detail":"No sports until symptom-free + CRP + troponin normalized — in myopericarditis at least 3-6 months."},{"naam":"Corticosteroids","detail":"Only in recurrent pericarditis or specific causes (autoimmune) — not in first episode (increases recurrence risk)."}]},{"_i":196,"q":"68-year-old man: tremor of right hand at rest, improves with movement, small handwriting. Parkinson's disease or essential tremor — what fits Parkinson's disease?","a":["Bilateral postural tremor, improves with alcohol","Resting tremor + bradykinesia + hypomimia + micrographia","Intention tremor + dysarthria + nystagmus","Tremor only with emotional stress"],"ex":"Parkinson's disease: resting tremor (pill-rolling, 4-6 Hz), bradykinesia, rigidity, postural instability, hypomimia, micrographia. Essential tremor: postural/action tremor, bilateral, improves with alcohol, no bradykinesia/rigidity. Resting tremor that improves with movement is characteristic of Parkinson's disease.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"The central distinction: Parkinson's disease has a resting tremor that improves with movement. Essential tremor is a postural tremor that appears with movement/holding posture. Bradykinesia + rigidity are exclusive to Parkinson's disease.","wiki_redflag":"Parkinson-plus (MSA, PSP): symmetric onset + early falls + poor levodopa response = poor prognostic sign. Always ask about autonomic symptoms and early falls.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Parkinson's disease: resting tremor","desc":"Dopamine deficiency in basal ganglia → abnormal oscillations in thalamus → tremor visible at rest (4-6 Hz, "pill-rolling"), disappears with intentional movement."},{"title":"Essential tremor: action tremor","desc":"Abnormal oscillations in olivo-cerebellar circuits → tremor with holding (postural) and movement, not at rest. Bilateral, hands and head."},{"title":"Bradykinesia","desc":"Characteristic of Parkinson's disease: slowing of movement initiation and speed. Micrographia (small handwriting) is an early sign."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Parkinson's disease","desc":"Resting tremor, asymmetric, bradykinesia, rigidity, hypomimia, micrographia. No intention tremor."},{"label":"Essential tremor","desc":"Postural/action tremor, bilateral, improves with alcohol, no bradykinesia/rigidity. Familial."},{"label":"Cerebellar tremor","desc":"Intention tremor (worse when approaching target), dysarthria, nystagmus, ataxia. Brainstem/cerebellar lesion."},{"label":"Parkinson-plus (MSA/PSP)","desc":"Parkinsonism + autonomic failure (MSA) or early backward falls/eye movement disorders (PSP). Poor levodopa response."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"No acute intervention — levodopa as diagnostic/therapeutic test when Parkinson's disease is suspected.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Levodopa test","detail":"Good response to levodopa confirms Parkinson's disease. Parkinson-plus: usually moderate or no response."},{"naam":"Essential tremor","detail":"Propranolol 40-320 mg/day or primidone 50-750 mg/day. Alcohol improvement is diagnostic but not treatment."},{"naam":"DBS","detail":"Deep brain stimulation of the subthalamic nucleus for disabling Parkinson tremor after medication optimization."}]},{"_i":197,"q":"34-year-old woman: 30 min visual shimmering scotoma, followed by throbbing hemicrania and nausea. TIA or migraine with aura — what supports migraine?","a":["Sudden maximal pain simultaneous with aura","Gradually spreading aura >5 min, followed by typical headache","Motor deficit, age >60 years, hypertension","Aura lasts >60 min without approaching headache"],"ex":"Migraine with aura: aura spreads gradually (cortical spreading depression), lasts 20-60 min, followed by ipsilateral headache. TIA: sudden deficit without spread, no subsequent headache, vascular risk factors. Gradual march + subsequent headache = migraine.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Migraine aura spreads gradually (the "march") over 20-60 minutes due to cortical spreading depression — TIA gives sudden, maximal deficit without progression. The subsequent headache after aura is characteristic of migraine.","wiki_redflag":"Excluding TIA is mandatory with: aura >60 min, motor deficit, age >50 years, cardiovascular risk factors, or atypical pattern. If in doubt: treat as TIA.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Cortical spreading depression (migraine)","desc":"Wave of depolarization spreads slowly (3 mm/min) across occipital cortex → "marching" scotomas that gradually expand."},{"title":"TIA: sudden embolus/thrombus","desc":"Sudden occlusion → maximal deficit immediately from the start, no gradual march. Resolves completely <24 h."},{"title":"Aura duration","desc":"Migraine aura: typically 20-60 min. Shorter (<5 min) or longer (>60 min) = atypical, consider TIA."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Migraine with aura","desc":"Aura gradually expands, 20-60 min, followed by headache. Young, female, no vascular risk factors. Previous identical episodes."},{"label":"TIA","desc":"Sudden maximal deficit (seconds), no march, no subsequent headache, older, vascular risk factors. Always exclude."},{"label":"Retinal migraine","desc":"Monocular visual loss (temporary), not binocular. Increased stroke risk — triptans contraindicated."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"If TIA is suspected: immediate ED referral, aspirin 300 mg, imaging.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Migraine with aura","detail":"Attack treatment: triptan + NSAID. Prophylaxis if frequent: propranolol or topiramate."},{"naam":"Contraceptive pill + aura","detail":"Migraine with aura + estrogen contraception = increased stroke risk. Switch to progesterone-only or non-hormonal method."}]},{"_i":198,"q":"30-year-old woman: two episodes of neurological deficit (once arm, once vision), MRI periventricular white matter. MS or NMO — what distinguishes MS from NMO?","a":["MS: longitudinally extensive myelitis >3 vertebrae","NMO: AQP4 antibodies negative","MS: short periventricular MRI lesions, AQP4-negative","NMO: same pattern as MS but more severe"],"ex":"MS: periventricular/juxtacortical lesions (short, <3 vertebrae myelitis), AQP4 antibodies negative, relapsing-remitting. NMO: longitudinal myelitis >3 vertebrae, area postrema lesions, AQP4-positive (80%), more severe than MS. Distinction is crucial: NMO responds poorly to MS DMTs.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"MS and NMO (neuromyelitis optica) can look clinically similar but are different diseases with different treatment. The distinction is crucial: NMO responds poorly (or worsens) with MS medication.","wiki_redflag":"MS-specific medication (natalizumab, interferon beta, fingolimod) can worsen NMO. Always test AQP4 antibodies before starting disease-modifying therapy.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"MS","desc":"T-cell mediated demyelination of CNS. Periventricular, juxtacortical and infratentorial lesions. Myelitis <3 vertebral bodies."},{"title":"NMO","desc":"AQP4 antibodies (aquaporin-4) damage astrocytes in optic nerves and spinal cord. Longitudinal myelitis >3 vertebrae, area postrema lesions (hiccups/vomiting = pathognomonic)."},{"title":"Treatment consequence","desc":"NMO: rituximab, eculizumab or inebilizumab. Some MS medications are contraindicated in NMO."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"MS","desc":"Short myelitis lesions (<3 vertebrae), periventricular MRI lesions, AQP4-negative, relapsing-remitting. Good response to MS DMTs."},{"label":"NMO (NMOSD)","desc":"Longitudinal myelitis >3 vertebrae, AQP4-positive (80%), more severe attacks, poor/dangerous response to MS DMTs."},{"label":"MOG antibody disease","desc":"MOG-IgG positive, AQP4-negative. Less severe than NMO. Distinction via serological panel."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Acute NMO relapse: high-dose methylprednisolone → plasma exchange if insufficient response.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"NMO prevention","detail":"Rituximab (anti-CD20), eculizumab or inebilizumab — proven effective in NMO."},{"naam":"Never in NMO","detail":"Natalizumab, fingolimod, alemtuzumab — contraindicated or can worsen NMO."},{"naam":"Serological panel","detail":"Test AQP4-IgG + MOG-IgG in every CNS demyelinating disease before starting treatment."}]},{"_i":199,"q":"LP result in 45-year-old man with fever and neck stiffness:\nGlucose 1.8 mmol/L (serum 5.2)\nProtein 3.8 g/L\n1400 cells/µL — 85% PMN\nBacterial or viral meningitis?","a":["Viral: lymphocytosis, glucose normal, protein low","Bacterial: PMN pleocytosis + glucose <40% serum + high protein","TB: identical picture but more chronic","Fungal: glucose low but lymphocytosis"],"ex":"Bacterial: PMN pleocytosis, glucose <40% serum (here 1.8/5.2=35%), protein >1 g/L, turbid fluid. Viral: lymphocytosis, glucose normal, protein slightly elevated (<1 g/L), clear. TB: lymphocytosis, low glucose, but slower. This result = bacterial → immediate empiric antibiotics (ceftriaxone + dexamethasone).","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":200,"q":"52-year-old man: acute dyspnea + pleuritic pain, no fever, no sputum. D-dimer 4.1, HR 118/min. Pneumonia or pulmonary embolism — what points to pulmonary embolism?","a":["Fever >38.5°C + purulent sputum + unilateral infiltrate","Pleuritic pain + tachycardia + high D-dimer + no signs of infection","Progressive symptoms over 3-5 days","Bilateral infiltrates + elevated CRP"],"ex":"Pulmonary embolism: sudden dyspnea, pleuritic pain, tachycardia, elevated D-dimer, absence of infectious signs (fever/sputum). Pneumonia: fever, productive cough, infiltrate, high CRP, more gradual. No infectious signs + high D-dimer = high Wells score → CT-PA indicated.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":201,"q":"T2DM patient, BMI 31, GFR 72, no heart failure. Metformin or sulfonylurea as first choice — what supports metformin?","a":["Stimulates insulin secretion, faster glucose reduction","No hypoglycemia risk, weight-neutral, proven cardiovascular benefit","Effective also with GFR <15","No gastrointestinal adverse effects"],"ex":"Metformin: reduces hepatic glucose production, no hypoglycemia risk, weight-neutral/reducing, UKPDS cardiovascular benefit. Sulfonylurea: promotes insulin secretion → hypoglycemia risk + weight gain. Cave metformin with GFR <30 (lactic acidosis risk). NHG: metformin = first choice in T2DM.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":202,"q":"HFrEF patient develops dry cough from lisinopril (ACE inhibitor). Stop and replace — ARB or ARNI as first step?","a":["ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan) directly as replacement","ARB (e.g. valsartan) as ACE inhibitor replacement — no cough, proven effectiveness","Increase beta-blocker and stop ACE inhibitor","Add digoxin for persistent symptoms"],"ex":"ACE inhibitor cough = bradykinin effect. ARB blocks AT1 receptor without bradykinin increase → no cough. Proven as ACE inhibitor replacement in HFrEF (CHARM-Alternative: candesartan). ARNI (sacubitril/valsartan) is the next step for further mortality reduction in a patient still optimizable.","dl":"Pharmacology","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":203,"q":"Acute kidney injury, creatinine increased:\nUrine Na 7 mmol/L\nUrine osmolality 680 mosm/kg\nFractional Na excretion 0.6%\nPrerenal or renal (ATN)?","a":["Renal (ATN): tubule cannot reabsorb Na, FeNa >2%","Prerenal: intact tubule, maximal Na reabsorption and urine concentration","Postrenal: hydronephrosis + elevated bladder pressure","ATN: Urine Na >40, isosthenuria (osmolality ~300)"],"ex":"Prerenal: intact tubule → maximal Na reabsorption (Urine Na <20, FeNa <1%) and urine concentration (osmolality >500). ATN (renal): tubular damage → Urine Na >40, FeNa >2%, osmolality ~300, granular casts. FeNa 0.6% + osmolality 680 = prerenal → fluid resuscitation.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":204,"q":"Microcytic anemia, MCV 70 fL:\nFerritin 165 µg/L\nTransferrin saturation 9%\nTIBC low\nIron deficiency anemia or anemia of chronic disease (ACD)?","a":["Iron deficiency: ferritin always low, TIBC high","ACD: ferritin normal/high (acute phase protein), low saturation, low TIBC","Thalassemia: normal ferritin and saturation, no anemia of disease","Sideroblastic anemia: ringed sideroblasts on bone marrow biopsy"],"ex":"Iron deficiency: ferritin LOW (<30), transferrin saturation low, TIBC HIGH. ACD: ferritin NORMAL/HIGH (is acute phase protein in infection/malignancy/RA), saturation low, TIBC LOW. Ferritin 165 + low saturation + low TIBC = ACD. Look for underlying cause (malignancy, chronic infection, inflammatory disease).","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":205,"q":"42-year-old woman: sudden "thunderclap headache", maximal intensity within seconds, neck stiffness, brain CT negative. Which test is now essential?","a":["Brain MRI with gadolinium","Lumbar puncture (LP) — bloody/xanthochromic CSF in SAH","EEG if epileptic seizure suspected","Carotid artery Doppler"],"ex":"CT misses 2-5% of SAH, especially early (<6 h) or small bleeds. With negative CT + strong suspicion of SAH (thunderclap headache), LP is mandatory: xanthochromia (yellow discoloration due to hemoglobin breakdown) proves SAH. MRI is less sensitive for acute blood than CT.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Thunderclap + negative CT = LP mandatory. CT has 98% sensitivity in first 6 h but drops to 85% after 24 h. LP after ≥6-12 h detects xanthochromia (yellow fluid due to hemoglobin breakdown) — diagnostic for SAH.","wiki_redflag":"Negative CT does NOT exclude SAH. 5% of SAH is missed on CT. No LP = missed SAH = risk of fatal rerupture.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"CT sensitivity decreases","desc":"Blood is progressively broken down → after 12-24 h CT signal decreases. CT positive in first 6 h in 98%, after 24 h only 85%."},{"title":"Xanthochromia","desc":"Hemoglobin breaks down into bilirubin in CSF → yellow discoloration visible after 6-12 h, remains present for 2 weeks. Spectrophotometry is more sensitive than visual inspection."},{"title":"LP timing","desc":"LP too early (<6 h): only fresh blood, may be traumatic tap. Wait at least 6-12 h after symptom onset."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"SAH — LP positive","desc":"Xanthochromia by spectrophotometry. Proves hemorrhage. Emergency neurosurgery/neuroradiology."},{"label":"SAH — LP negative","desc":"Xanthochromia absent ≥12 h after onset of symptoms = SAH excluded. Consider other thunderclap causes (RCVS, CVT)."},{"label":"Traumatic LP","desc":"Fresh blood in first tube, clearing in subsequent tubes. No xanthochromia. Artifact."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"LP after ≥6-12 h → xanthochromia spectrophotometry → if positive: angiography (CTA or DSA) → aneurysm treatment.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"CTA cerebral vessels","detail":"After confirming SAH: CTA to localize aneurysm (98% sensitive). DSA if CTA negative with strong clinical suspicion."},{"naam":"Nimodipine","detail":"60 mg orally 4x/day for 21 days — reduces vasospasm and ischemic risk day 4-14."},{"naam":"Rerupture prevention","detail":"Coiling or clipping within 24-72 h. 30% rerupture risk without treatment, mortality 70%."}]},{"_i":206,"q":"28-year-old man: two separate demyelinating episodes, symptoms >24 h, periventricular white matter on MRI. Suspected MS — which test adds the most diagnostically?","a":["EEG: slow-wave activity in MS","LP: oligoclonal bands in CSF confirm intrathecal immunoglobulin production","PET scan: white matter metabolism","EMG: neurogenic patterns in demyelination"],"ex":"MRI is first choice in MS (McDonald criteria). LP with oligoclonal bands (present in >95% of MS, not in serum) confirms intrathecal inflammation when MRI is not fully diagnostic. Visual evoked potentials (VEP) can also contribute with optic nerve involvement. EEG has no role in MS.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"After MRI, LP with oligoclonal bands (OCB) is the most valuable additional test in MS. OCB in CSF (not serum) are present in >95% of MS patients and can confirm "dissemination in time" — allowing earlier diagnosis.","wiki_redflag":"OCB are not MS-specific — also in neuroborreliosis, neurosyphilis, NMO and other neuroinflammation. Always determine AQP4 antibodies to exclude NMO.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"McDonald criteria","desc":"MS diagnosis requires dissemination in space (≥2 CNS locations) and time (≥2 episodes or new MRI lesions). CSF OCB can replace "dissemination in time"."},{"title":"OCB as "dissemination in time"","desc":"Presence of OCB with first clinical attack + MRI lesions sufficient for MS diagnosis (McDonald 2017) — without waiting for a second attack."},{"title":"EEG: no role","desc":"EEG has no diagnostic value in MS. Relevant for epilepsy diagnostics, not demyelinating diseases."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"CSF OCB in MS","desc":"Sensitivity >95%. Specificity moderate — always combine with MRI and clinical picture. No OCB = MS unlikely."},{"label":"VEP (visual evoked potential)","desc":"Delayed P100 wave proves subclinical optic nerve involvement — useful in monosymptomatic onset."},{"label":"AQP4 antibodies","desc":"Negative in MS, positive in NMO (80%). Always determine in atypical presentation or longitudinal myelitis."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Confirm MS diagnosis → immediately discuss disease-modifying therapy (DMT) to reduce relapses.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"First-line DMT","detail":"Interferon beta, glatiramer acetate, dimethyl fumarate — for mild-moderate MS."},{"naam":"Second-line DMT","detail":"Natalizumab, ocrelizumab — for active or rapidly progressive MS."},{"naam":"Relapse","detail":"Methylprednisolone 1 g IV for 3-5 days in acute relapse with functional loss."}]},{"_i":207,"q":"22-year-old young man: seizure with loss of consciousness, tongue bite, incontinence, 2 minutes. Epilepsy or syncope — which test distinguishes best?","a":["Brain CT: always first step","EEG after seizure for interictal abnormalities + tilt test/Holter for syncope","Blood glucose during seizure","Sleep study: sleep apnea as cause"],"ex":"Epilepsy vs syncope: EEG (interictal epileptiform activity, sensitivity 50-60%) + history (tongue bite/incontinence points to epilepsy). Syncope: tilt test (orthostatic/vasovagal), Holter (cardiac). Tongue bite + incontinence + postictal confusion = epilepsy likely. MRI afterwards for structural cause.","dl":"Neurology","wiki_kern":"Tongue bite (lateral!) + incontinence + postictal confusion = epilepsy. EEG detects interictal activity (sensitivity 50-60% with first EEG, higher with sleep EEG). Syncope: Holter + tilt test.","wiki_redflag":"Not every "convulsion" is epilepsy. Syncope with muscle jerks (convulsive syncope) is often confused with epilepsy. Key distinction: in syncope no postictal confusion.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Epilepsy features","desc":"Lateral tongue bite (side — midline suggests other trauma), urinary incontinence, tonic-clonic >1-2 min, postictal confusion/sleep."},{"title":"Syncope features","desc":"Brief (<30 sec), triggered (standing, emotion, pain), brief muscle jerks possible but no tongue bite, rapid recovery without confusion."},{"title":"EEG value","desc":"Interictal epileptiform activity on first EEG: sensitivity 50-60%. Sleep EEG or repeat EEG: higher sensitivity. Normal EEG does NOT exclude epilepsy."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Epilepsy","desc":"Lateral tongue bite + incontinence + postictal confusion/sleep + EEG abnormality. MRI for structural cause."},{"label":"Convulsive syncope","desc":"Muscle jerks during loss of consciousness but brief, no tongue bite, rapid recovery, ECG/tilt test positive."},{"label":"Non-epileptic seizure (PNES)","desc":"Psychogenic seizure. No EEG correlate. Diagnosis via video-EEG. Treatment: psychotherapy."},{"label":"Cardiac syncope","desc":"Sudden unconsciousness without aura, ECG abnormality, family history of sudden death. Holter/ILR mandatory."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"First seizure: measure blood glucose, request EEG + MRI. Inform about driving ban.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"EEG","detail":"As soon as possible after the seizure (within 24 h more sensitive). Sleep EEG if awake EEG negative. Video-EEG if doubt PNES vs epilepsy."},{"naam":"MRI","detail":"Brain MRI (epilepsy protocol) after first seizure in adult — always exclude structural cause."},{"naam":"Antiepileptic drug","detail":"With second seizure or high recurrence risk: levetiracetam or lamotrigine. Driving ban until 1 year seizure-free."}]},{"_i":208,"q":"58-year-old man: pressing chest pain 20 min, radiation to left arm, sweating. Suspected ACS — what do you do as first diagnostic step?","a":["CT coronary angiography: coronary anatomy","ECG within 10 minutes + high-sensitivity troponin (0 h and 1 h/3 h)","Stress echo: provoke ischemia","Holter: exclude arrhythmia"],"ex":"Guideline: ECG within 10 minutes after arrival (STEMI = direct cath lab). High-sensitivity troponin on arrival + 1 h or 3 h later (0/1 h protocol or 0/3 h). This algorithm rules out or confirms >99% of NSTEMI/ACS. CT coronary in stable patient with lower suspicion.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"ACS diagnostics follow a strict time protocol. ECG within 10 minutes identifies STEMI for direct PCI. Hs-troponin at 0 h and 1-3 h later rules out or confirms NSTEMI. This algorithm has a negative predictive value of >99.5%.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":"minutes","wiki_bigfact_sub":"ECG time target after arrival with suspected ACS — in STEMI activate cath lab immediately","wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"ECG: immediate decision","desc":"STEMI (ST elevation ≥1 mm in ≥2 contiguous leads) = immediate PCI, no further diagnostics needed. No STEMI but symptoms: start NSTEMI protocol."},{"title":"Hs-troponin time window","desc":"Troponin leaks out after myocardial injury → measurable in blood after 1-3 hours. One measurement is insufficient. Serial (delta-troponin) measurement: rise or fall of ≥52% over 1 h = rule-in ACS."},{"title":"0/1 h vs 0/3 h protocol","desc":"0/1 h algorithm (ESC 2020): faster triage. Measurable rise in 1 hour = rule-in. No rise + low absolute level = rule-out. 0/3 h with unclear hs-TnT baseline."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"ECG + hs-troponin 0/1 h protocol","desc":"First choice with suspected ACS: ECG <10 min, troponin on arrival + 1 h. More sensitive and faster than conventional troponin protocol."},{"label":"CT coronary angiography","desc":"For stable patients with low-to-moderate pre-test probability of coronary artery disease (outside the acute phase). Not suitable as first step in acute ACS."},{"label":"Exercise test/stress echo","desc":"Only in stable patients with doubtful symptom pattern after excluding acute MI. Never in the acute presentation phase."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"In STEMI: activate cath lab while ECG is still being made — do not lose a minute.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"NSTEMI/uncertain","detail":"Aspirin + heparin + determine GRACE score → invasive strategy (coronary angiography) within 24-72 h depending on risk score."},{"naam":"Rule-out after 0/1 h protocol","detail":"Negative hs-troponin at 0 h AND 1 h + no ECG abnormalities + low HEART score → discharge safely possible."}]},{"_i":209,"q":"68-year-old woman: dyspnea, ankle edema, elevated JVP. Suspected heart failure — BNP/NT-proBNP or echocardiography as first test?","a":["Echocardiography always first: definitive diagnosis","BNP/NT-proBNP first: fast, high negative predictive value — normal virtually excludes heart failure","Chest X-ray: wet lungs = proof of heart failure","24 h urinary cortisol: exclude adrenal insufficiency"],"ex":"BNP/NT-proBNP: high negative predictive value. Normal BNP (<35 pg/mL) makes heart failure unlikely → no echo needed. Elevated BNP → echo for confirmation (systolic/diastolic) and treatment guidance. Heart failure + normal BNP = consider another cause of dyspnea.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"BNP (brain natriuretic peptide) and NT-proBNP are released by cardiomyocytes with increased wall stress (volume or pressure overload). Negative BNP (<35 pg/mL for BNP, <125 pg/mL for NT-proBNP) virtually excludes heart failure as a cause of dyspnea.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":"negative predictive value","wiki_bigfact_sub":"normal BNP (<35 pg/mL) with suspected heart failure in the acute setting","wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Synthesis and release","desc":"Ventricular wall stretched by elevated filling pressure or increased volume → pro-BNP split → active BNP + inactive NT-proBNP. BNP: active natriuresis and vasodilation. NT-proBNP: longer half-life (~70 h vs ~20 min BNP) → more stable level."},{"title":"BNP specificity","desc":"BNP/NT-proBNP elevated in heart failure, but also in: renal failure, sepsis, PE, COPD exacerbation, older age. Specificity ~70% → elevated BNP = suspicion of heart failure, but confirmation by echo needed."},{"title":"Echo after elevated BNP","desc":"Echocardiography determines type of heart failure (HFrEF EF <40% vs HFpEF EF ≥50%), underlying cause (valvular disease, wall motion abnormalities) and treatment guidance."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"BNP/NT-proBNP first: exclusion of heart failure","desc":"Normal BNP makes heart failure unlikely — cheap, fast. Elevated BNP → echo for confirmation and specification."},{"label":"Echocardiography: definitive diagnosis","desc":"Confirms heart failure, determines EF, filling pressures, valvular disease. Indicated after elevated BNP or with strong clinical suspicion despite normal BNP."},{"label":"Chest X-ray in heart failure","desc":"Pulmonary congestion, Kerley B lines, cardiomegaly — supportive but not diagnostic. Specific in acute pulmonary edema but less sensitive."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Acute heart failure with elevated BNP + clinical features: IV furosemide + oxygen.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"BNP as treatment target","detail":"BNP-guided dose adjustment of diuretics reduces recurrent hospital admissions (PRIMA study). Target: BNP reduction >30% after treatment."},{"naam":"Echo for therapy choice","detail":"HFrEF vs HFpEF determines the medication strategy — echo is indispensable for this."}]},{"_i":210,"q":"Patient with fever, new heart murmur and positive blood cultures (S. aureus). Suspected endocarditis — TTE or TEE?","a":["TTE always sufficient: good image quality in all patients","TEE: higher sensitivity (>90%) for vegetations and complications, always in high-risk or negative TTE","Chest CT: detect peripheral emboli","Blood cultures alone sufficient for diagnosis"],"ex":"TTE (transthoracic): first choice, non-invasive, sensitivity 60-75%. TEE (transesophageal): sensitivity >90%, indication with: negative TTE but high clinical suspicion, prosthetic valves, S. aureus bacteremia, aortic valve involvement, looking for complications. Duke criteria: two major (blood cultures + echo) = definitive.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Endocarditis diagnostics: TTE is the first step (non-invasive, fast), but TEE is essential in high-risk patients (S. aureus, prosthetic valve, negative TTE with high clinical suspicion). TEE has sensitivity >90% vs 60-75% for TTE.","wiki_redflag":"S. aureus bacteremia = TEE always mandatory, even with negative TTE. S. aureus also adheres to intact valves and rapidly causes valve destruction — missing endocarditis is fatal. Consider surgical indications early.","wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Duke criteria","desc":"Major: ≥2 positive blood cultures of typical pathogen + echocardiographic evidence (vegetation/abscess/perforation). Minor: predisposition, fever, vascular phenomena, immunological signs. Definite: 2 major, or 1 major + 3 minor."},{"title":"TEE vs TTE","desc":"TTE: external probe, lung tissue/ribs/fat attenuate the image. TEE: probe in esophagus directly behind the heart → no attenuation → higher resolution, detection of vegetations <3 mm, abscesses in aortic root/annular region."},{"title":"TEE indications","desc":"Negative TTE with high clinical suspicion, prosthetic valves (TTE almost always insufficient), S. aureus bacteremia, intracardiac device (ICD/pacemaker), clinical deterioration."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"TEE in high-risk endocarditis","desc":"S. aureus bacteremia, negative TTE with high suspicion, prosthetic valve, aortic valve involvement. Sensitivity >90% for vegetations and complications (abscess, fistula)."},{"label":"TTE as first step","desc":"Non-invasive, widely available. Sufficient in: low suspicion of endocarditis, good image quality, native mitral/aortic valve without complications. Followed by TEE if positive or in doubt."},{"label":"CT chest/abdomen","desc":"Additional for peripheral septic emboli, aortic involvement, surgical planning. Not for diagnosis of endocarditis."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Start empiric antibiotics after obtaining at least 2 sets of blood cultures — never delay for echocardiography.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Antibiotics (S. aureus MSSA)","detail":"Flucloxacillin 12 g/day IV for 6 weeks."},{"naam":"Surgical indications","detail":"Heart failure, uncontrolled infection, embolism prophylaxis (vegetation >10 mm + high-risk pathogen). Endocarditis team decides multidisciplinary."}]},{"_i":211,"q":"Patient with fatigue, weight gain, cold intolerance. Evaluate thyroid function — which test is the best first step?","a":["Free T4 (fT4): direct thyroid level measurement","TSH: most sensitive marker of thyroid function, elevated in hypothyroidism","Anti-TPO antibodies: autoimmune cause","T3: only elevated in T3 toxicosis"],"ex":"TSH is the best screening test: the pituitary is extremely sensitive to small changes in thyroid hormone status. Elevated TSH = hypothyroidism (also subclinical). Normal TSH = thyroid virtually certainly intact. Only if TSH abnormal, then measure free T4 and possibly free T3.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":212,"q":"Patient with jaundice, dark urine, clay-colored stools, ALT 420 U/L. Cholestatic vs hepatocellular — which test best makes the distinction?","a":["Liver biopsy: always first step in jaundice","Abdominal ultrasound: biliary obstruction vs parenchymal liver disease","PET scan: exclude malignancy","Bile acid measurement: primary biliary cholangitis"],"ex":"Abdominal ultrasound is the first step in jaundice: dilated bile ducts = extrahepatic obstruction (stone, malignancy) → ERCP. No dilation = intrahepatic cause (hepatitis, cirrhosis). Liver biopsy only after excluding obstruction. Blood tests (ALT/ALP ratio) give direction but ultrasound is anatomically decisive.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":213,"q":"Patient with bleeding tendency: easy bruising, gum bleeding. PT elevated, APTT normal, platelets normal. Which coagulation problem fits this?","a":["Thrombocytopenia: low platelets","Extrinsic cascade disorder — vitamin K deficiency or liver function disorder (PT elevated, APTT normal)","Von Willebrand disease: APTT elevated","Hemophilia A: factor VIII deficiency, APTT elevated"],"ex":"PT measures the extrinsic cascade (factor VII, X, V, II, I). APTT measures the intrinsic cascade (VIII, IX, XI). PT elevated + APTT normal = disorder in extrinsic pathway → vitamin K deficiency (factor VII shortest half-life), VKA use, or early liver failure. Hemophilia and vWD give APTT elevation.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":214,"q":"65-year-old man: fever, cough, right lower lobe infiltrate. CAP (community-acquired pneumonia) — which test identifies the pathogen fastest?","a":["Sputum culture: always reliable and fast","Urinary antigen (pneumococcus + legionella): fast result, also if antibiotics already started","Bronchoscopy with BAL: always gold standard","Blood culture only in severe sepsis"],"ex":"Urinary antigen (pneumococcus and legionella): result within hours, not affected by antibiotic use — always obtain if uncertain or severe pneumonia. Sputum culture: reliable but slow (48-72 h), obtain on admission. Blood culture: low sensitivity (5-14%) but always in severe CAP (CURB-65 ≥3).","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":215,"q":"Patient with fever, neck stiffness, photophobia. Suspected bacterial meningitis — immediate LP or first brain CT?","a":["Always CT first, then LP — CT is safer","LP immediately if no signs of increased intracranial pressure (focal neurological, decreased consciousness, papilledema) — do not delay antibiotics","Wait for PCR result before LP","MRI is better than CT for LP indication assessment"],"ex":"LP may be done immediately if no signs of increased ICP (normal consciousness, no papilledema, no focal deficit). CT first with: focal deficit, decreased consciousness, seizure, immunocompromised. CRUCIAL: never delay antibiotics for LP or CT. Start ceftriaxone + dexamethasone immediately, perform LP as soon as possible afterwards.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":216,"q":"Patient with fever and clinical sepsis, suspected bacteremia. When to obtain blood cultures for optimal yield?","a":["Always after starting antibiotics: bacteria then more detectable","Before starting antibiotics, preferably during or just before fever spike — two sets from different sites","Only if temperature >39°C: otherwise too little bacteremia","One set sufficient: duplicate is wasteful"],"ex":"Blood cultures BEFORE antibiotics: antibiotics drastically lower sensitivity. Timing during or just before fever spike (during bacteremic phase). Two sets (aerobic + anaerobic) from two different sites: increases sensitivity from 80% to >90% and distinguishes contaminant from true bacteremia. Do not delay treatment in severe sepsis.","dl":"Infectious diseases","wiki_kern":null,"wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":null,"wiki_onderscheid":null,"wiki_therapie_urgent":null,"wiki_therapie_stappen":null},{"_i":217,"q":"68-year-old man with sudden palpitations and mild dizziness. Which rhythm is seen on this ECG?","a":["Sinus tachycardia","Atrial fibrillation","Atrial flutter 2:1","Ventricular tachycardia"],"ex":"Absolutely irregular QRS rhythm without P waves = atrial fibrillation. Note the irregular RR intervals and the chaotic fibrillatory baseline. In atrial flutter there are sawtooth F waves with a regular rhythm (~150/min). Risk of thrombus in left atrial appendage → anticoagulation.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Atrial fibrillation (AF) is the most common arrhythmia. Multiple chaotic re-entry circuits in the atria (400-600/min) make coordinated contraction impossible.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Chaotic atrial activity","desc":"Multiple re-entry circuits in the atria (400-600/min) — coordinated atrial contraction is impossible. Left atrial appendage barely contracts."},{"title":"Thrombus formation","desc":"Stasis of blood in the left atrial appendage → thrombus formation → embolization to brain or other organs. This is why anticoagulation is the cornerstone of treatment."},{"title":"Irregular ventricular rhythm","desc":"AV node conducts randomly from the chaotic atrial impulses → absolutely irregular RR rhythm. No P waves on ECG — only fibrillatory baseline."},{"title":"CHA₂DS₂-VASc risk stratification","desc":"Anticoagulation indication: men ≥2 points, women ≥3 points. Points for: heart failure, hypertension, age ≥75 (2pt), DM, stroke/TIA (2pt), vascular disease, age 65-74, female sex."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Atrial fibrillation","desc":"Absolutely irregular RR rhythm, no P waves, chaotic fibrillatory baseline. Anticoagulation based on CHA₂DS₂-VASc score."},{"label":"Atrial flutter","desc":"Regular ~150/min, sawtooth F waves in II/III/aVF, carotid massage makes F waves visible."},{"label":"Sinus tachycardia","desc":"Regular, clear P wave before each QRS, rate varies with respiration."},{"label":"AVNRT","desc":"Paroxysmal, regular narrow-complex, sudden start/stop, P hidden in or just after QRS."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Acutely hemodynamically unstable: immediate electrical cardioversion.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Rate control","detail":"Beta-blocker (metoprolol) or non-DHP calcium channel blocker (diltiazem) — first choice in permanent AF."},{"naam":"Rhythm control","detail":"Flecainide (no structural heart disease) or amiodarone — cardioversion after ≥3 weeks anticoagulation or TEE to exclude thrombus."},{"naam":"Anticoagulation","detail":"DOAC (apixaban, rivaroxaban, dabigatran) based on CHA₂DS₂-VASc. VKA with mechanical valve or mitral stenosis."},{"naam":"Pulmonary vein ablation","detail":"Curative in symptomatic paroxysmal AF — isolation of ectopic foci in pulmonary veins."}]},{"_i":218,"q":"58-year-old patient with 40 minutes of pressing retrosternal pain. Which diagnosis does this ECG fragment show?","a":["Pericarditis","Inferior STEMI","NSTEMI","Normal ECG"],"ex":"Localized ST elevation in the inferior leads (II, III, aVF) fits an inferior STEMI due to RCA occlusion. Immediate PCI within 90 minutes! The dashed line shows the baseline — the ST segment is clearly above it. Pericarditis gives diffuse saddle-shape ST elevation in ALL leads.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Inferior STEMI (leads II, III, aVF) is caused by occlusion of the right coronary artery (RCA). ST elevation shows transmural infarction — every minute of delay = more myocardial damage.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Plaque rupture","desc":"Atherosclerotic plaque in the RCA ruptures → subendothelial collagen exposed → platelet aggregation."},{"title":"Thrombus formation","desc":"Complete occlusion of the RCA by a thrombus → transmural inferior myocardium ischemic."},{"title":"ST elevation mechanism","desc":"Ischemic cells depolarize abnormally → current loop toward adjacent healthy tissue → ST elevation in inferior leads (II, III, aVF)."},{"title":"Reciprocal depression","desc":"Opposite leads (I, aVL) show mirror-image ST depression — confirms localization and is typical of STEMI (absent in pericarditis)."},{"title":"RV infarct risk","desc":"Proximal RCA also supplies the RV → always obtain V4R. ST elevation ≥1 mm in V4R = RV infarct → do not give nitrates or diuretics!"}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Inferior STEMI (RCA occlusion)","desc":"ST elevation in II, III and aVF. Reciprocal depression in I and aVL. Always obtain right-sided ECG (V4R) to exclude RV infarction."},{"label":"Pericarditis","desc":"Diffuse saddle-shape ST elevation in all leads, PR depression, no reciprocal depressions, posture-dependent pain."},{"label":"NSTEMI","desc":"No ST elevation, but troponin elevated. ST depression and T inversion possible."},{"label":"Early repolarization","desc":"Concave (scoop-shaped) ST elevation in young patients without symptoms — benign variant."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Immediate PCI within 90 minutes — every minute of delay = more myocardial loss.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Dual antiplatelet therapy","detail":"Aspirin 300 mg + ticagrelor 180 mg immediately on arrival."},{"naam":"Heparin IV","detail":"Bridging until PCI — prevents further thrombus growth in the RCA."},{"naam":"V4R always","detail":"In inferior STEMI: right-sided ECG leads to exclude RV infarction — this changes management (no nitrates)."},{"naam":"Secondary prevention","detail":"Statin + beta-blocker + ACE inhibitor + SGLT2 inhibitor after discharge."}]},{"_i":219,"q":"Which conduction disturbance pattern does this ECG show (note the PR intervals)?","a":["First-degree AV block","Wenckebach (Mobitz type I)","Mobitz type II AV block","Third-degree AV block"],"ex":"Wenckebach (Mobitz I): PR interval becomes longer with every beat until one QRS drops — then resets. Recognizable by the progressive PR prolongation and the periodically dropped QRS. Benign, typical with inferior wall infarction or increased vagal tone. Mobitz II: sudden QRS drop without PR prolongation — more dangerous.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"Wenckebach is a second-degree AV block in which the AV node becomes "fatigued" after each conducted beat → progressively longer PR → ultimately an impulse that is not conducted. Then the AV node recovers and the cycle starts again.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Causes","desc":"AV nodal pathology due to inferior wall ischemia (RCA), increased vagal tone (athletes, sleep) or digoxin toxicity."},{"title":"The Wenckebach pattern","desc":"Cyclic periods: first beat short PR, each following beat longer PR, until the impulse does not pass through the AV node (QRS drops) → then reset to short PR. Example: 3:2 block = 2 QRS for 3 P waves."},{"title":"Benign character","desc":"Wenckebach is at AV node level — the escape rhythm if blocked is junctional (~50/min), hemodynamically sufficient. Mobitz II (His bundle level) is more dangerous (ventricular escape ~30/min)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Wenckebach (Mobitz I)","desc":"Progressively longer PR → QRS drop → reset. Benign, AV nodal phenomenon, typical with inferior MI or digoxin toxicity."},{"label":"Mobitz II","desc":"Constant PR interval until sudden QRS drop — His bundle damage, more serious, can quickly cause complete block → pacemaker indication."},{"label":"First-degree AV block","desc":"PR >200 ms but every P conducts → asymptomatic, no treatment needed."},{"label":"Third-degree AV block","desc":"P and QRS completely dissociated, ventricular escape rhythm ~35/min — urgent pacemaker always."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Wenckebach in inferior MI: observation, reversible. Wenckebach with digoxin: stop digoxin.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Symptomatic","detail":"Atropine 0.5 mg IV in symptomatic bradycardia — increases AV nodal conduction."},{"naam":"Underlying cause","detail":"Treat inferior MI (PCI), stop culprit medication (digoxin, beta-blocker, calcium channel blocker)."},{"naam":"Pacemaker rarely needed","detail":"Wenckebach is usually benign and reversible — permanent block is rare in Wenckebach."}]},{"_i":220,"q":"72-year-old patient with presyncope and pulse rate 35/min. Which rhythm does this rhythm strip show?","a":["Wenckebach (Mobitz I)","Mobitz type II AV block","Complete (third-degree) AV block","Sick sinus syndrome"],"ex":"Complete AV block: P waves (blue, 75/min) and QRS complexes (35/min) are completely dissociated — no signal from the atria reaches the ventricles. The ventricles beat at their own rhythm via an escape pacemaker in the His-Purkinje system. Life-threatening — urgent temporary pacemaker.","dl":"Cardiology","wiki_kern":"In third-degree (complete) AV block, no atrial impulse conducts to the ventricles. The atria and ventricles beat independently: atria ~75/min, ventricles ~35/min via junctional or ventricular escape rhythm.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Causes","desc":"Inferior MI (temporary, junctional escape ~50/min, reversible after PCI), anterior MI (His bundle damage, ventricular escape ~30/min, poor prognosis), degenerative fibrosis (Lenegre, in older adults), aortic valve surgery, digitalis toxicity."},{"title":"Escape rhythm","desc":"Ventricles beat at their own pace via a low escape center: junctional ~50/min (narrow QRS, hemodynamically better) or ventricular ~30/min (wide QRS, hemodynamically unstable). Unreliable → syncope, hemodynamic collapse."},{"title":"Hemodynamic consequences","desc":"Low cardiac output due to slow ventricular rate → hypotension, presyncope/syncope, angina. Atria and ventricles beat completely independently — AV dissociation is pathognomonic."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Complete (third-degree) AV block","desc":"P waves (~75/min) and QRS escape rhythm (~35/min) completely dissociated — no atrial impulse reaches the ventricles. Urgent pacemaker."},{"label":"Wenckebach (Mobitz I)","desc":"Some P waves conducted, PR progressively prolonged → QRS drop → reset. Benign."},{"label":"Mobitz II","desc":"Constant PR until sudden QRS drop — also serious but P-QRS relationship still exists."},{"label":"Sinus bradycardia","desc":"Slow but P and QRS always correlated with normal PR interval."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Transcutaneous pacemaker as bridge in hemodynamic instability — isoprenaline infusion as alternative.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Permanent pacemaker","detail":"Definitive treatment in symptomatic or irreversible complete AV block."},{"naam":"Reversible cause","detail":"Inferior STEMI: temporary block, recovery after PCI. Digitalis toxicity: stop medication, Fab antibodies in severe toxicity."}]},{"_i":221,"q":"The orange curve shows the spirogram of a 65-year-old smoker. Which interpretation fits best?","a":["Normal spirogram","Restrictive pattern (FVC reduced, FEV1/FVC normal)","Obstructive pattern (FEV1/FVC reduced)","Mixed pattern"],"ex":"Obstructive pattern: the orange curve has a lower peak flow and a concave (inwardly curved) expiratory limb — characteristic of flow limitation in COPD or asthma. FEV1/FVC ratio <0.70 confirms obstruction. Restrictive pattern: narrower loop, lower FVC, but normally shaped curve.","dl":"Laboratory","wiki_kern":"The flow-volume curve shows expiratory flow (Y-axis) plotted against volume (X-axis). In COPD: dynamic airway compression during expiration → lower peak flow, concave curve.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"COPD: alveolar destruction (emphysema) + airway inflammation."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"elastic recoil ↓ + airway resistance ↑."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"air trapping (RV enlarged) + flow limitation. The concave curve occurs because flow decreases early as the small airways already collapse."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Obstructive pattern (COPD)","desc":"FEV1/FVC <0.70 after bronchodilation, concave expiratory curve. In smoker: COPD. FEV1 decline determines severity (GOLD stages I-IV)."},{"label":"Obstructive pattern (Asthma)","desc":"Also FEV1/FVC reduced, but reversible: FEV1 rises ≥12% and ≥200 ml after bronchodilator — distinguishes asthma from COPD."},{"label":"Restrictive pattern (fibrosis, obesity)","desc":"FVC low, FEV1/FVC normal or increased, curve smaller but normal in shape. TLC <80% confirms restriction."},{"label":"Mixed pattern","desc":"Reduced FEV1/FVC and reduced TLC — obstruction and restriction simultaneously (e.g. COPD + obesity)."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"COPD exacerbation: SABA (salbutamol) + ipratropium nebulizer + prednisolone 30 mg orally + antibiotics if infectious.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Stable COPD","detail":"Smoking cessation (most effective), SABA/LABA, LAMA (tiotropium), ICS with recurrent exacerbations."},{"naam":"Oxygen","detail":"If SpO₂ <92%: dose to target 88-92% in COPD — too much O₂ can worsen hypercapnia."},{"naam":"Pulmonary rehabilitation","detail":"Proven effective for quality of life and exercise tolerance."}]},{"_i":222,"q":"65-year-old man, 40 pack-years smoking. Progressive exertional dyspnea, cough with sputum. Spirometry after bronchodilator: FEV1/FVC 0.62, FEV1 58% predicted. Most likely diagnosis?","a":["COPD","Asthma","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Bronchiectasis"],"ex":"COPD: postbronchodilator FEV1/FVC <0.70 in a smoker with compatible symptoms. In asthma there is significant reversibility (>12% FEV1 increase). IPF gives a restrictive pattern (FVC low, FEV1/FVC normal or increased).","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"COPD is a chronic, progressive airway disease with not fully reversible obstruction. Two pathomechanisms: chronic bronchitis (hypersecretion) and emphysema (alveolar destruction).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Tobacco smoke."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"inflammation in small airways."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"fibrosis and narrowing + alveolar destruction."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"reduced elastic recoil."},{"title":"Step 5","desc":"air trapping. FEV1/FVC ratio falls. GOLD stage I-IV based on FEV1% predicted."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Asthma","desc":"younger age, atopy, variability, reversible."},{"label":"IPF","desc":"restrictive pattern, bibasal crackles, clubbing, honeycombing pattern on HRCT."},{"label":"Bronchiectasis","desc":"daily purulent sputum production, recurrent infections."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Step 1: SABA (salbutamol) for symptoms.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"LAMA (tiotropium)."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"LAMA+LABA or LABA+ICS."},{"naam":"With exacerbations","detail":"prednisolone + antibiotics."},{"naam":"Step 4","detail":"Smoking cessation slows FEV1 decline."}]},{"_i":223,"q":"34-year-old woman, sudden dyspnea and right-sided pleuritic chest pain, pulse 108/min. D-dimer 2.1 mg/L, Wells score 5 points. Which investigation is now indicated first?","a":["CT pulmonary angiography","Chest X-ray","V/Q scintigraphy","Echocardiography"],"ex":"With high clinical suspicion (Wells ≥5), immediate CT-PA is indicated. D-dimer has no added value as an exclusion test with high pre-test probability. CT-PA is the gold standard for diagnosing pulmonary embolism.","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"Pulmonary embolism (PE): thrombus in the pulmonary circulation, usually originating from deep venous thrombosis. The Wells score estimates the clinical suspicion.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Thrombus."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"increased pulmonary vascular resistance."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"acute right ventricular overload. Small emboli: pleuritic pain due to peripheral infarction. Large emboli: hypotension, syncope, cardiogenic shock."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Pneumothorax","desc":"also sudden dyspnea but hyperresonant percussion and absent breath sounds."},{"label":"Pneumonia","desc":"fever, productive cough, infiltrate on chest X-ray."},{"label":"Pericarditis","desc":"posture-dependent pain, friction rub."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Immediate anticoagulation: LMWH, then DOAC (rivaroxaban or apixaban).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Massive PE with shock","detail":"consider thrombolysis."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"At least 3 months anticoagulation."}]},{"_i":224,"q":"22-year-old man, acute-onset dyspnea and left-sided pleuritic pain. Percussion: hyperresonant left. Breath sounds absent on the left. Most likely diagnosis?","a":["Spontaneous pneumothorax","Pulmonary embolism","Pleural effusion","Pneumonia"],"ex":"Spontaneous pneumothorax: hyperresonant percussion + absent breath sounds ipsilaterally = air in the pleural space. Typical in tall, thin young men due to rupture of subpleural bullae. Pleural effusion instead gives dullness to percussion.","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"Primary spontaneous pneumothorax in young thin men due to rupture of subpleural bullae at the lung apex. Risk of recurrence: 30-50% after first episode.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Bulla rupture."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"air in pleural space."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"lung collapses. Tension pneumothorax: valve effect."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"mediastinum shifts contralaterally."},{"title":"Step 5","desc":"compression of great vessels."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Pleural effusion","desc":"dullness to percussion, reduced breath sounds."},{"label":"Atelectasis","desc":"dullness, trachea deviates ipsilaterally."},{"label":"Pneumonia","desc":"fever, bronchial breathing, crackles."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Small and stable: observation + oxygen (accelerates resorption).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Symptomatic","detail":"aspiration or chest tube."},{"naam":"Tension pneumothorax","detail":"immediate needle decompression 2nd ICS midclavicular line without waiting for chest X-ray."}]},{"_i":225,"q":"Which mechanism explains the bronchodilating effect of tiotropium in COPD?","a":["Blockade of the M3 muscarinic receptor in the bronchial wall","Beta-2 receptor agonism in bronchial smooth muscle cells","Inhibition of phosphodiesterase-4 → cAMP increase","Inhibition of leukotriene synthesis via 5-lipoxygenase"],"ex":"Tiotropium is a LAMA (Long-Acting Muscarinic Antagonist). It blocks the M3 receptor → relaxation of bronchial smooth muscle cells → bronchodilation. Once-daily dosing, 24-hour action.","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"Acetylcholine via the M3 receptor causes bronchoconstriction and mucus production. LAMAs block this → bronchodilation and less secretion.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"LAMA action","desc":"Blocks M3 receptor in bronchial muscle cells → relaxation → bronchodilation. Improves FEV1, reduces hyperinflation and exacerbation frequency."},{"title":"Adverse effects","desc":"Dry mouth, urinary retention (cave: prostate problems), constipation, narrow-angle glaucoma."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"SABA (salbutamol)","desc":"Beta-2 agonist, fast-acting (5-15 min), rescue use in acute dyspnea."},{"label":"LABA (salmeterol/formoterol)","desc":"Beta-2 agonist, long-acting (12 h), maintenance medication — combine with LAMA in severe COPD."},{"label":"ICS","desc":"Inhibits airway inflammation, no direct bronchodilation. Add with frequent exacerbations."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Acute COPD exacerbation: SABA + ipratropium nebulizers, oxygen O2 sat 88-92%, oral corticosteroids.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"COPD maintenance therapy","detail":"LAMA (tiotropium) as first-choice monotherapy in stable COPD. With persistent dyspnea: LAMA + LABA combination."},{"naam":"Severe COPD (FEV1<50%)","detail":"Triple therapy: LAMA + LABA + ICS — significantly reduces exacerbation frequency."},{"naam":"Smoking cessation","detail":"Only intervention that slows FEV1 decline. Always combine with pharmacological treatment."}]},{"_i":226,"q":"Which antibiotic is first choice in outpatient community-acquired pneumonia (CAP) without comorbidities in an adult?","a":["Oral amoxicillin","Oral ciprofloxacin","Azithromycin monotherapy","Intravenous ceftriaxone"],"ex":"Outpatient CAP without comorbidities: amoxicillin 500 mg 3×/day. Streptococcus pneumoniae is the most frequent pathogen and is well sensitive to amoxicillin. Ciprofloxacin poorly covers S. pneumoniae and is not a standard choice for CAP.","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"S. pneumoniae causes 30-40% of all CAP cases. Amoxicillin has excellent pneumococcal coverage and is first choice in outpatient CAP without comorbidities.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"CURB-65","desc":"Determines severity and admission policy: Confusion, Urea >7 mmol/L, Respiratory rate ≥30, Blood pressure <90 mmHg systolic, age ≥65."},{"title":"Score 0-1","desc":"Treat outpatient — amoxicillin 500 mg 3×/day 5 days."},{"title":"Score ≥3","desc":"Hospital admission + IV antibiotics (amoxicillin/clavulanic acid or ceftriaxone + macrolide)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Amoxicillin (first choice)","desc":"Excellent pneumococcal coverage, oral, inexpensive. Outpatient CAP without comorbidities."},{"label":"Ciprofloxacin","desc":"Poor activity against S. pneumoniae — NOT as monotherapy for CAP."},{"label":"Levofloxacin","desc":"Effective, but higher adverse-effect threshold (tendon rupture, QT prolongation). Reserve for complications."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Start antibiotics within 4 hours after diagnosis — delayed start increases mortality.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Outpatient CURB-65 0-1","detail":"Amoxicillin 500 mg 3×/day 5 days."},{"naam":"Admission CURB-65 2","detail":"Amoxicillin 4×/day IV or oral co-amoxiclav, consider adding macrolide if atypical pathogen suspected."},{"naam":"ICU CURB-65 ≥3","detail":"Piperacillin/tazobactam IV + macrolide or levofloxacin. Test Legionella urinary antigen."}]},{"_i":227,"q":"58-year-old woman, never smoked. Months-long dry cough and progressive dyspnea. Bibasal end-inspiratory crackles and clubbing. HRCT: bibasal honeycombing pattern. Diagnosis?","a":["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","COPD","Sarcoidosis","Extrinsic allergic alveolitis"],"ex":"IPF: progressive fibrosis in older adults (never smoked), bibasal crackles, clubbing and honeycombing pattern (UIP pattern) on HRCT. Sarcoidosis more often gives hilar lymphadenopathy in younger patients.","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"IPF is a chronic fibrosing interstitial lung disease with poor prognosis (median survival 3-5 years). UIP pattern on HRCT (honeycombing + traction bronchiectasis bibasally) is diagnostic without biopsy in classic presentation.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Repeated microtrauma to the alveolar epithelium."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"abnormal wound healing."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"fibroblast activation."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"collagen deposition."},{"title":"Step 5","desc":"loss of gas-exchange surface. Restrictive lung function pattern: FVC reduced, FEV1/FVC normal, DLCO reduced."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"EAA (extrinsic allergic alveolitis)","desc":"history of antigen exposure (birds, molds), younger, partly reversible."},{"label":"Sarcoidosis","desc":"bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy, granulomas."},{"label":"NSIP","desc":"younger women, less honeycombing, better prognosis."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Pirfenidone and nintedanib slow FVC decline.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 1","detail":"No corticosteroids in IPF."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"Lung transplantation in suitable patients."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"Early palliative care and oxygen with hypoxemia."}]},{"_i":228,"q":"28-year-old young man. Bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy on chest X-ray, erythema nodosum on the legs, joint pain. No fever. Diagnosis?","a":["Sarcoidosis (Löfgren syndrome)","Tuberculosis","Non-Hodgkin lymphoma","Mycoplasma pneumonia"],"ex":"Löfgren syndrome: the classic triad of bilateral hilar lymphadenopathy + erythema nodosum + joint pains is pathognomonic for acute sarcoidosis. Prognosis excellent: >80% spontaneous remission.","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"Sarcoidosis is a multisystem disease with non-caseating granulomas. Löfgren syndrome is the acute presentation, typical in women of childbearing age and Northern European men.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Unknown trigger."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"CD4+ T-helper cell activation."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"granuloma formation. Elevated serum ACE and hypercalcemia (due to 1,25-OH-vitamin D production in granulomas) are suggestive but not specific."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Tuberculosis","desc":"also hilar nodes but asymmetric, fever, night sweats, weight loss, positive Quantiferon."},{"label":"Lymphoma","desc":"asymmetric, B symptoms."},{"label":"Berylliosis","desc":"occupational exposure to beryllium."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Löfgren: NSAID for joint symptoms, expectant management given good prognosis.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Severe or persistent sarcoidosis","detail":"prednisone."},{"naam":"Chronic","detail":"methotrexate or hydroxychloroquine as steroid-sparing agent."}]},{"_i":229,"q":"Patient with HIV (CD4+ 55 cells/μL), fever, dry cough and progressive dyspnea over 2 weeks. Saturation 88%. Chest X-ray: bilateral butterfly-shaped infiltrates. Most likely diagnosis?","a":["Pneumocystis jirovecii pneumonia (PCP)","Bacterial pneumonia","Tuberculosis","CMV pneumonitis"],"ex":"PCP occurs with severe immunosuppression (CD4+ <200/μL). Classic: subacute course, dry cough, progressive dyspnea, low saturation, bilateral butterfly-shaped infiltrates. LDH elevated. Treatment: cotrimoxazole (TMP-SMX).","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"Pneumocystis jirovecii is a fungus that is latently present in immunocompetent individuals. With CD4+ <200/μL: risk of severe pneumonia.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"P. jirovecii infects alveoli."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"trophozoites fill alveolar space."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"impaired gas exchange."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"hypoxemia. Elevated LDH is a marker of lung parenchymal damage."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"TB in HIV","desc":"fever, night sweats, weight loss, cavities or miliary abnormalities."},{"label":"CMV pneumonitis","desc":"also in severe HIV."},{"label":"Bacterial pneumonia","desc":"more acute, lobar, productive cough."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"TMP-SMX (cotrimoxazole) for 21 days.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"In moderate/severe PCP (PaO2 <70 mmHg)","detail":"adding prednisolone reduces mortality."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"Start ART after stabilization."}]},{"_i":230,"q":"62-year-old man, severe pneumonia not responding to amoxicillin. Bilateral infiltrates on chest X-ray. Legionella urinary antigen positive. Which antibiotic is now first choice?","a":["Intravenous levofloxacin","Intravenous amoxicillin","Intravenous benzylpenicillin","Intravenous cotrimoxazole"],"ex":"Legionella pneumophila is an intracellular bacterium. Beta-lactam antibiotics (amoxicillin, penicillin) are ineffective because they do not enter the cell. First choice: fluoroquinolone (levofloxacin) or macrolide (azithromycin), preferably fluoroquinolone in severe presentation.","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"Legionella survives in alveolar macrophages — standard beta-lactams do not reach the intracellular location. Classic triad: high fever + relative bradycardia + hyponatremia. Source: contaminated water (cooling towers, shower systems).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Intracellular bacterium","desc":"Legionella multiplies in macrophages → beta-lactams (amoxicillin, penicillin) that do not penetrate cells are ineffective."},{"title":"Atypical picture","desc":"Elevated LDH, liver enzyme abnormalities, renal insufficiency, hyponatremia. Bilateral pneumonia, sometimes rapidly progressive."},{"title":"Urinary antigen","desc":"Serogroup 1 (80% of cases) detectable in urine — fast and sensitive. With negative antigen but strong suspicion: culture on BCYE agar."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Legionella","desc":"High fever, hyponatremia, elevated LDH, bilateral. Urinary antigen positive. Levofloxacin/azithromycin."},{"label":"Mycoplasma","desc":"Younger patient, milder course, cold agglutinins, macrolide sensitive. Sometimes outbreaks in school/barracks."},{"label":"S. pneumoniae","desc":"Most frequent, penicillin-sensitive, no hyponatremia, no LDH rise."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Levofloxacin 500 mg 2×/day IV — first choice in severe Legionella pneumonia.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Duration","detail":"10-14 days (longer than standard CAP — to prevent recurrence)."},{"naam":"Alternative","detail":"Azithromycin 500 mg 1×/day IV in milder presentation."},{"naam":"Notifiable disease","detail":"Legionella is notifiable — source investigation mandatory to stop spread."}]},{"_i":231,"q":"Arterial blood gas in COPD exacerbation: pH 7.28, pCO2 72 mmHg, pO2 50 mmHg, HCO3- 32 mmol/L. Which interpretation is correct?","a":["Respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation","Respiratory alkalosis with metabolic compensation","Mixed metabolic and respiratory acidosis","Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation"],"ex":"pH decreased (7.28) + pCO2 elevated (72) = respiratory acidosis as primary disorder. HCO3- elevated (32) = chronic renal compensation. This is the classic acute-on-chronic CO2 retention pattern in COPD.","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"Acid-base stepwise approach: (1) low pH = acidosis. (2) high pCO2 = respiratory cause. (3) high HCO3- = chronic renal compensation. Expected HCO3 in chronic respiratory acidosis: +3.5 mmol per 10 mmHg pCO2 increase.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Alveolar hypoventilation","desc":"CO2 accumulates → carbonic acid → pH falls (respiratory acidosis)."},{"title":"Chronic compensation","desc":"Kidneys retain HCO3- to buffer pH (days). HCO3- +3.5 mmol/10 mmHg pCO2 increase."},{"title":"Acute-on-chronic","desc":"Patient already had elevated HCO3- due to chronic CO2 retention. Acute exacerbation = pH falls further while HCO3- is already high."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Respiratory acidosis","desc":"pH low, pCO2 high, HCO3- high (compensatory). Cause: hypoventilation (COPD, sedation, neuromuscular)."},{"label":"Metabolic acidosis","desc":"pH low, HCO3- low, pCO2 low (compensatory). Cause: DKA, renal failure, lactic acidosis."},{"label":"Respiratory alkalosis","desc":"pH high, pCO2 low. Cause: hyperventilation, sepsis, liver insufficiency."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"NIV (non-invasive ventilation) with pH <7.35 and pCO2 >45 — reduces need for intubation and mortality in COPD exacerbation.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Controlled oxygen","detail":"O2 saturation target 88-92% in COPD with CO2 retention. Venturi mask 24-28% FiO2."},{"naam":"Bronchodilators","detail":"SABA + SAMA nebulizers. Systemic corticosteroids (prednisolone 40 mg/day 5 days)."},{"naam":"Antibiotics","detail":"With purulent sputum or fever: amoxicillin or doxycycline 5 days."}]},{"_i":232,"q":"45-year-old woman, recurrent respiratory infections, daily purulent sputum production, chronic cough. Chest CT: cylindrical dilated airways bibasally. Diagnosis?","a":["Bronchiectasis","COPD","Asthma","Pulmonary fibrosis"],"ex":"Bronchiectasis: irreversible dilation of the airways visible on CT as "signet ring sign" (airway larger than accompanying vessel) and tram-track lines. Characteristic: daily purulent sputum production and recurrent infections.","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"Bronchiectasis results from destruction of the airway wall by chronic infection and inflammation → irreversible dilation → secretion stasis → further infection (vicious circle). Most frequent pathogens: H. influenzae, P. aeruginosa (advanced stage).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"HRCT is the gold standard."},{"title":"Look for causes","desc":"CF (sweat chloride test), primary ciliary dyskinesia (PCD), immunodeficiency (Ig levels), post-tuberculosis, post-infectious."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"Idiopathic in 50%."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"COPD","desc":"emphysema + obstruction but no cylindrical dilation on CT."},{"label":"Asthma","desc":"reversible, no structural CT abnormalities."},{"label":"CF","desc":"also bronchiectasis but with pancreatic insufficiency and elevated sweat chloride."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Airway clearance (PEP, autogenic drainage).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Exacerbations","detail":"amoxicillin/co-amoxiclav; P. aeruginosa: ciprofloxacin."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"Chronic macrolide (azithromycin 3×/week) with frequent exacerbations."}]},{"_i":233,"q":"Which clinical feature most reliably distinguishes asthma from COPD in a patient with airway obstruction?","a":["Significant reversibility after bronchodilator (FEV1 increase >12% and >200 mL)","Wheezing on auscultation","Age under 40 years","Nocturnal symptoms and cough"],"ex":"Reversibility is the key physiological criterion: in asthma, FEV1 normalizes or improves significantly after salbutamol (>12% and >200 mL). In COPD, obstruction is by definition not fully reversible. Wheezing and nocturnal symptoms can occur in both.","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"The central distinction between asthma and COPD is reversibility. Asthma: FEV1 rises >12% and >200 mL after bronchodilator. COPD: persistent obstruction (FEV1/FVC <0.70 postbronchodilator).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Asthma","desc":"Eosinophilic inflammation, IgE-mediated or non-atopic, intermittent. Airway hyperreactivity. Reversible."},{"title":"COPD","desc":"Neutrophilic inflammation, tobacco smoke, emphysema + chronic bronchitis. Persistently progressive. Irreversible."},{"title":"ACOS","desc":"Overlap in smokers with atopy — features of both. Treat both components."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Asthma","desc":"Young, atopy, reversibility after salbutamol, FeNO elevated, nocturnal symptoms, no smoking history."},{"label":"COPD","desc":"Smoker >10 pack-years, >40 years, persistent obstruction, emphysema on CT, no significant reversibility."},{"label":"ACOS","desc":"Smoker with atopy, partly reversible. Treat both: ICS + LABA + LAMA."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Acute asthma attack: repeated SABA (salbutamol) + O2 + systemic prednisolone.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Asthma stepwise scheme","detail":"Step 1: SABA rescue. Step 2: low-dose ICS. Step 3: ICS + LABA. Step 4: high ICS + LABA ± LAMA. Step 5: add-on biologicals (omalizumab, mepolizumab)."},{"naam":"COPD GOLD stages","detail":"GOLD A: LAMA or LABA. GOLD B: LAMA + LABA. Frequent exacerbations: +ICS or macrolide."}]},{"_i":234,"q":"What is the most frequent cause of chronic cough (>8 weeks) in a non-smoking adult without abnormalities on chest X-ray?","a":["Postnasal drip (upper airway cough syndrome)","COPD","Lung carcinoma","Bronchiectasis"],"ex":"The "big three" causes of chronic cough: (1) postnasal drip/UACS (~40%), (2) cough-variant asthma (~25%), (3) GERD (~20%). Postnasal drip is the most frequent cause. Always also exclude ACE inhibitor as iatrogenic cause.","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"The "big three" causes of chronic cough in non-smokers: (1) postnasal drip/UACS (~40%), (2) cough-variant asthma (~25%), (3) GERD (~20%). Always exclude ACE inhibitors as iatrogenic cause.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Postnasal drip (UACS)","desc":"Nasal secretions drip into pharynx → irritation of cough reflex. Treat rhinitis/sinusitis."},{"title":"Cough-variant asthma","desc":"Cough as only symptom, no dyspnea. Confirm with bronchoprovocation test (methacholine)."},{"title":"ACE inhibitor cough","desc":"Bradykinin accumulation → cough reflex in 5-20% of users. Stop ACE inhibitor → cough disappears in 4-8 weeks."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Postnasal drip","desc":"Sensation of dripping behind throat, blowing nose, allergy history. Treat with antihistamines + nasal steroids."},{"label":"ACE inhibitor cough","desc":"Dry cough, began after starting ACE inhibitor. Stop medication → cough disappears. Replace with ARB."},{"label":"Lung carcinoma","desc":"Smoker, older, hemoptysis, weight loss — always exclude! Chest X-ray/CT mandatory."},{"label":"GERD","desc":"Cough worse after meal or lying down. Heartburn not required ("silent reflux")."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Stop ACE inhibitor if medication cause — replacement with ARB (no bradykinin effect).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Postnasal drip","detail":"Nasal steroids (mometasone) + non-sedating antihistamines. Escalate if persistent."},{"naam":"Cough-variant asthma","detail":"ICS trial treatment 4-6 weeks. Positive response confirms diagnosis."},{"naam":"GERD","detail":"PPI 2×/day 8-week trial. No late meals, raise head of bed."}]},{"_i":235,"q":"In a COPD patient with chronic CO2 retention, 100% oxygen should always be given during an exacerbation to normalize saturation as quickly as possible.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. COPD patients with chronic hypercapnia partly rely on hypoxic respiratory drive. High oxygen concentrations can reduce respiratory drive → CO2 retention worsens. Target SpO2: 88-92% (Venturi mask 24-28%).","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"In chronic hypercapnia, central chemoreceptors become desensitized to CO2. 100% oxygen in COPD with CO2 retention reduces hypoxic respiratory drive → hypoventilation → pCO2 rises further. Goal: SpO2 88-92%.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Hypoxic drive","desc":"Peripheral (carotid) chemoreceptors respond to O2 — with high FiO2 this signal decreases → reduced respiratory drive → hypoventilation."},{"title":"Haldane effect","desc":"Oxygen displaces CO2 from hemoglobin → free CO2 rises in blood. Contributes to CO2 retention with high FiO2."},{"title":"Venturi mask","desc":"Delivers accurate 24-28% O2 independent of respiratory rate — suitable for controlled oxygen therapy."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"COPD with chronic hypercapnia","desc":"Target SpO2 88-92%. Venturi mask 24-28%. Monitor pCO2 via blood gas."},{"label":"Other patients (no CO2 retention)","desc":"Normalize saturation >94%. No danger of CO2 retention with acute oxygen use."},{"label":"NIV"}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Controlled O2 via Venturi mask 24-28% — SpO2 target 88-92%. Repeat blood gas after 30-60 min.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"NIV with acidosis","detail":"pH <7.35 + pCO2 >45: non-invasive ventilation (BIPAP) — reduces need for intubation in COPD exacerbation."},{"naam":"Avoid high O2","detail":"No non-rebreather mask in chronic CO2 retention — risk of hypoventilation spiral."}]},{"_i":236,"q":"A negative D-dimer excludes pulmonary embolism in a patient with high clinical suspicion (Wells score ≥7).","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. D-dimer is an exclusion test that is only useful with low or intermediate clinical suspicion. With high suspicion (Wells ≥7), PE prevalence is high — perform immediate CT pulmonary angiography, do not obtain D-dimer.","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"D-dimer has high sensitivity but low specificity — useful only with low or intermediate clinical suspicion. Wells ≥7 = direct CT-PA, omit D-dimer. Negative D-dimer with low suspicion has NPV >99%.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Bayesian reasoning","desc":"With high pre-test probability (Wells ≥7), PE prevalence is high. A moderately sensitive test such as D-dimer can then NOT exclude the diagnosis."},{"title":"Wells score algorithm","desc":"Score <2: apply PERC rule or D-dimer. Score 2-6: D-dimer. Score ≥7: direct CT-PA."},{"title":"D-dimer elevated in","desc":"Infection, trauma, cancer, pregnancy, older age, postoperative state — low specificity makes it unusable as a confirmation test."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"CT-PA","desc":"Gold standard with high or intermediate Wells score — shows localization, extent and alternatives (pneumonia, pericardium)."},{"label":"V/Q scintigraphy","desc":"Alternative with contrast hypersensitivity (allergy), renal failure or pregnancy (less radiation)."},{"label":"Lower limb ultrasound","desc":"Additional with suspected DVT. Positive DVT + clinical suspicion of PE = treat without CT."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"High Wells + hemodynamically unstable: systemic thrombolysis (alteplase) or catheter embolectomy.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Anticoagulants","detail":"LMWH (enoxaparin) SC as bridging. Start DOAC (apixaban 10 mg 2×/day for 7 days, then 5 mg 2×/day) — simpler than VKA."},{"naam":"Treatment duration","detail":"3 months in provoked PE (surgery). 6 months in idiopathic. Lifelong with recurrence or malignancy."}]},{"_i":237,"q":"Asthma is characterized by irreversible airway obstruction that does not respond to bronchodilators.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Asthma is by definition reversible airway obstruction. An FEV1 increase >12% and >200 mL after bronchodilator is characteristic. Irreversible obstruction is the distinguishing feature of COPD.","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"Asthma is by definition reversible airway obstruction. Reversibility testing: FEV1 rises >12% and >200 mL after salbutamol. COPD: persistent obstruction even after bronchodilator (FEV1/FVC <0.70).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Reversibility test","desc":"Measure FEV1 before and 15 min after salbutamol 400 mcg. Increase >12% and >200 mL = significant reversibility → asthma."},{"title":"Remodeling","desc":"Long-standing untreated asthma can become partly irreversible due to structural airway change. Early treatment prevents this."},{"title":"PEF variability","desc":"Daily PEF measurement >20% variation over 2 weeks confirms asthma — simple diagnostic tool."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Asthma","desc":"Reversible, FEV1 normal between attacks, atopy, young, FeNO elevated."},{"label":"COPD","desc":"FEV1/FVC <0.70 persistent despite bronchodilator. Progressive, smoker, older."},{"label":"Vocal cord dysfunction (VCD)","desc":"Inspiratory stridor, variable, normal spirometry outside attack. Treatment: speech therapy."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Severe asthma attack: salbutamol nebulizer every 20 min + ipratropium + prednisolone 40 mg + O2.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Intermittent (step 1)","detail":"SABA on demand. No maintenance therapy needed."},{"naam":"Mild persistent (step 2)","detail":"Low-dose ICS daily + SABA rescue."},{"naam":"Moderate-severe (step 3-4)","detail":"ICS + LABA combination. If insufficient control: add high ICS + LAMA."}]},{"_i":238,"q":"Pleural fluid in a patient with heart failure meets Light's criteria for an exudate.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Heart failure causes a transudate: increased hydrostatic pressure → protein-poor fluid. Light's criteria for exudate: fluid/serum protein >0.5, or LDH ratio >0.6, or LDH >2/3 of upper limit normal. Heart failure meets none of these criteria.","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"Pleural fluid classification with Light's criteria: exudate if fluid/serum protein >0.5, LDH ratio >0.6, or LDH >2/3 upper limit normal. Heart failure causes a transudate — does not meet Light's criteria.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Transudate mechanism","desc":"Heart failure → elevated pulmonary capillary pressure → protein-poor fluid leaks into pleural cavity. Low protein concentration → Light's criteria negative."},{"title":"Pseudo-exudate","desc":"After diuretics for heart failure: fluid becomes concentrated → Light's criteria can become positive (pseudo-exudate). Use albumin gradient: serum-pleural albumin >1.2 g/dL → still heart failure."},{"title":"Exudate causes","desc":"Pneumonia, malignancy, tuberculosis, pulmonary embolism (increased vascular permeability)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Heart failure (transudate)","desc":"Light's criteria negative, bilateral, responds to diuretics. Cave pseudo-exudate."},{"label":"Tuberculosis (exudate)","desc":"Lymphocytosis, high ADA (>40 U/L), unilateral, young patient. ADA test is fast and cheap."},{"label":"Malignancy (exudate)","desc":"Bloody, cytology positive, high LDH. Unilateral, weight loss, smoking."},{"label":"Chylothorax","desc":"Triglycerides >1.24 mmol/L in pleural fluid — chyle duct damaged (trauma, lymphoma)."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Large effusion with dyspnea: therapeutic thoracentesis (drainage 1-1.5 L max per session).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Heart failure","detail":"Diuretics (furosemide). Optimize heart failure — effusion disappears when heart failure recovers."},{"naam":"Parapneumonic","detail":"Antibiotics for pneumonia. With loculated exudate with low pH (<7.20): drainage via chest tube."},{"naam":"Malignant effusion","detail":"Repeated thoracentesis or pleurodesis (talc powder) for palliative drainage."}]},{"_i":239,"q":"Lung cancer is the most common cause of cancer death worldwide in both men and women.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer death worldwide in both men and women. The 5-year survival is <20% due to late diagnosis: 75% present with advanced stage.","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"Lung cancer: NSCLC (85%) versus SCLC (15%). NSCLC: adenocarcinoma (also non-smokers, EGFR mutations), squamous cell (smokers, central). SCLC: almost exclusively smokers, rapidly growing, early metastasis, paraneoplastic syndromes.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Adenocarcinoma","desc":"Peripheral, also in non-smokers (Asian women: EGFR mutations in 40-60%). Targeted therapy: erlotinib, gefitinib, osimertinib."},{"title":"Squamous cell carcinoma","desc":"Central, strongly related to smoking, hemoptysis. Cavitation possible. Squamous cell carcinoma marker."},{"title":"SCLC","desc":"Rapidly growing, early metastasis. Paraneoplastic syndromes: SIADH, ectopic ACTH (Cushing), Eaton-Lambert."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"NSCLC (adenocarcinoma)","desc":"Peripheral, early-stage surgery. EGFR mutation: TKI. PD-L1 positive: immunotherapy (pembrolizumab)."},{"label":"SCLC","desc":"Chemotherapy + radiotherapy. Surgery rarely possible. Rapid recurrence. Paraneoplastic syndromes."},{"label":"Metastases","desc":"Lung metastases from other primary tumor (breast, colon, kidney) — CT/PET for distinction."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Hemoptysis in lung cancer: CT angiography, possibly bronchial artery embolization.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Stage I-II NSCLC","detail":"Surgical resection (lobectomy) — curative. Adjuvant chemotherapy with positive nodes."},{"naam":"Stage III-IV NSCLC","detail":"Mutation testing: EGFR, ALK, ROS1, PD-L1. Targeted therapy or immunotherapy + chemotherapy."},{"naam":"Screening","detail":"Low-dose CT in smokers >20 pack-years and age 50-80 years — reduces mortality 20% (NLST study)."}]},{"_i":240,"q":"In which patient is spirometry the most appropriate first-line diagnostic investigation?","a":["55-year-old smoker with progressive exertional dyspnea and cough","22-year-old with acute pleuritic pain and dyspnea","Patient with high fever, productive cough and lobar infiltrate","Patient with hemodynamic instability and tachycardia 120/min"],"ex":"Spirometry is the diagnostic tool for chronic airway diseases (COPD, asthma) in patients with chronic symptoms. In acute presentations (pneumothorax, pulmonary embolism, pneumonia), other investigations have priority. Spirometry is not useful in the acute setting.","dl":"Pulmonology","wiki_kern":"Spirometry is the diagnostic tool for chronic airway obstruction. COPD diagnosis requires postbronchodilator FEV1/FVC <0.70. Asthma: reversibility test >12% and >200 mL. Not useful in acute setting.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Obstructive pattern","desc":"FEV1/FVC <0.70 postbronchodilator = COPD. FEV1 reduced, FVC normal or slightly reduced."},{"title":"Restrictive pattern","desc":"FVC reduced, FEV1/FVC normal or increased. Causes: pulmonary fibrosis, obesity, pleural effusion, neuromuscular disease."},{"title":"COPD GOLD staging","desc":"Based on postbronchodilator FEV1: GOLD 1 FEV1 ≥80%, GOLD 2 50-80%, GOLD 3 30-50%, GOLD 4 <30%."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Spirometry","desc":"Chronic symptoms, suspected COPD/asthma, preoperative. Not for acute presentations."},{"label":"Chest X-ray","desc":"Acute presentation, structural abnormalities, cardiomegaly. First step in acute."},{"label":"CT-PA","desc":"Suspected pulmonary embolism, emphysema evaluation, malignancy."},{"label":"Blood gas","desc":"Acute acid-base problem, oxygenation, CO2 retention. Additional to spirometry in severe COPD."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Spirometry is diagnostic — no direct therapeutic implication other than making diagnosis.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"After COPD diagnosis","detail":"Start LAMA or LABA monotherapy. Smoking cessation as priority. Pulmonary rehabilitation."},{"naam":"After asthma diagnosis","detail":"ICS as maintenance therapy. Repeat reversibility test after 4-6 weeks of treatment."},{"naam":"Preoperative","detail":"FEV1 <1 L: increased perioperative risk. FEV1 <0.8 L: high risk → anesthesiology consultation."}]},{"_i":241,"q":"55-year-old man, 4 weeks of melena, has used ibuprofen for 2 months. Hemoglobin 7.4 g/dL. Most likely cause?","a":["NSAID-induced gastric ulcer","Colorectal carcinoma","Esophageal varices","Colonic diverticulosis"],"ex":"NSAID use is the most frequent iatrogenic cause of upper GI bleeding. NSAIDs inhibit COX-1 → reduced prostaglandin synthesis → weakened gastric mucosal barrier → ulcer formation. Melena = blood from upper GI tract.","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"Melena (black tarry stool) occurs due to oxidation of hemoglobin in the intestine, indicating that the bleeding source is above the transverse colon. Gastric and duodenal ulcers together are the most common cause of upper GI bleeding.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"NSAIDs: COX-1 inhibition."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"prostaglandin E2 synthesis decreases."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"gastric mucosa is not protected (less mucus and bicarbonate, reduced blood flow). Risk factors for NSAID ulcer: age >65 years, H. pylori co-infection, high NSAID dose, concomitant corticosteroid use."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Colorectal carcinoma","desc":"more often red blood with stool or occult blood loss + weight loss."},{"label":"Esophageal varices","desc":"with liver cirrhosis, sudden hematemesis."},{"label":"Diverticulosis","desc":"lower GI, more often bright red blood, no melena."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Stop NSAID.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 1","detail":"IV PPI (esomeprazole or pantoprazole)."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"Urgent endoscopy within 24 h."},{"naam":"With active bleeding","detail":"endoscopic hemostasis (clips, coagulation)."},{"naam":"Step 4","detail":"H. pylori eradication if detected."}]},{"_i":242,"q":"28-year-old woman, bloody diarrhea >6 weeks, abdominal cramps, weight loss. Colonoscopy: continuous inflammation extending proximally from rectum, no skip lesions. Diagnosis?","a":["Ulcerative colitis","Crohn's disease","Infectious colitis","Ischemic colitis"],"ex":"Ulcerative colitis: continuous inflammation starting in the rectum, extending proximally, no skip lesions, only mucosa affected. Crohn's disease: discontinuous inflammation (skip lesions), transmural inflammation, can occur anywhere in the gut.","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"IBD: chronic immune-mediated intestinal inflammations. UC is limited to the mucosa of the colon, always with rectal involvement.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"UC","desc":"mucosal ulcerations, crypt abscesses, pseudopolyps."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"Risk of colorectal carcinoma after 8-10 years of active UC → surveillance colonoscopy."},{"title":"Crohn's disease","desc":"mucosal abnormalities, fistulas, strictures, perianal disease."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Infectious colitis","desc":"acute course, culture positive (Campylobacter, Salmonella, C. diff)."},{"label":"Ischemic colitis","desc":"older adults, vascular risk factors, splenic flexure distribution."},{"label":"Microscopic colitis","desc":"watery diarrhea, normal colonoscopy appearance."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"UC remission induction: 5-ASA (mesalazine) for mild/moderate disease; corticosteroids in moderate/severe course.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Maintenance","detail":"5-ASA."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"Biologics (infliximab, vedolizumab) with treatment resistance."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"Surgery (colectomy) in severe or treatment-resistant UC."}]},{"_i":243,"q":"45-year-old man, acute epigastric pain radiating to the back, nausea, vomiting. Amylase 850 U/L (normal <100). Most likely cause?","a":["Acute pancreatitis due to gallstones or alcohol","Gastric perforation","Mesenteric ischemia","Aortic dissection"],"ex":"Acute pancreatitis: elevated amylase >3× normal with epigastric pain radiating to the back. Most common causes: gallstones (45%) and alcohol (35%). Diagnosis with ≥2 of 3 criteria: typical pain, amylase/lipase >3× normal, imaging.","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"Amylase and lipase rise in pancreatitis. Lipase is more specific (amylase also elevated in renal insufficiency, salivary gland abnormalities, intestinal ischemia).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Early activation of trypsin."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"auto-digestion of the pancreas."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"local and systemic inflammation. Gallstone pancreatitis: stone blocks pancreatic duct."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"obstruction. Alcohol pancreatitis: direct toxicity to acinar cells."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Gastric perforation","desc":"rigid abdomen, pneumoperitoneum on chest X-ray."},{"label":"Mesenteric ischemia","desc":"older, vascular risk factors, pain worse than findings."},{"label":"Aortic dissection","desc":"tearing pain, blood pressure asymmetry."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Conservative: nil per os initially, aggressive IV fluid supplementation (Ringer lactate preferred), analgesia.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Gallstone pancreatitis","detail":"ERCP with biliary obstruction + cholecystectomy during same admission."},{"naam":"Infected necrosis","detail":"antibiotics + drainage."}]},{"_i":244,"q":"62-year-old man, gradually increasing jaundice, weight loss, painless. Abdominal ultrasound: dilated bile ducts, hypoechoic mass in pancreatic head. Most likely diagnosis?","a":["Pancreatic carcinoma","Choledocholithiasis","Primary sclerosing cholangitis","Hepatocellular carcinoma"],"ex":"Painless obstructive jaundice + mass in pancreatic head is classic for pancreatic carcinoma. Courvoisier sign: palpable, non-tender gallbladder with jaundice points to malignant obstruction (not gallstones, which give a contracted gallbladder).","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"Pancreatic carcinoma: 5-year survival <10% due to late diagnosis. Adenocarcinoma of ductal epithelium (95%).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Obstruction of the common bile duct."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"bile cannot reach intestine."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"conjugated bilirubin rises in blood."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"jaundice, dark urine, pale stool. Dilation of bile ducts and gallbladder due to obstruction (Courvoisier)."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Choledocholithiasis","desc":"painful jaundice, fever (Charcot triad), gallstones on ultrasound."},{"label":"PSC","desc":"bile duct strictures and dilatations, associated with ulcerative colitis."},{"label":"HCC","desc":"with liver cirrhosis, AFP elevated."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Resectable: Whipple procedure (pancreaticoduodenectomy).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Palliative","detail":"biliary drainage via ERCP (stent), chemotherapy (FOLFIRINOX or gemcitabine+nab-paclitaxel)."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"Poor prognosis in metastatic disease."}]},{"_i":245,"q":"By which mechanism do proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) reduce gastric acid production?","a":["Irreversible blockade of H+/K+-ATPase in parietal cells","Blockade of H2 receptors on parietal cells","Neutralization of gastric acid by basic compounds","Inhibition of gastrin release by G cells"],"ex":"PPIs (omeprazole, pantoprazole) irreversibly block H+/K+-ATPase (proton pump) in parietal cells → most effective inhibition of gastric acid production. H2 blockers (ranitidine) block H2 receptors but are less effective.","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"PPIs are prodrugs activated in the acidic environment of the parietal cell. After activation they bind covalently to H+/K+-ATPase → irreversible inhibition until new pump is synthesized (24-48 h). Most effective acid suppression.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"H+/K+-ATPase blockade","desc":"PPI is activated at pH <2 → covalent binding to cysteine of the proton pump → irreversible inhibition. Taken daily before meal (pump then active)."},{"title":"H2 blockers (cimetidine)","desc":"Inhibit histamine-stimulated acid production — less effective than PPIs. Faster effect but shorter duration."},{"title":"Long-term use","desc":"Hypomagnesemia, vitamin B12 deficiency, increased Clostridioides difficile risk, osteoporosis. Use minimum effective dose."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"PPI indications","desc":"GERD, gastric ulcer, H. pylori eradication (triple therapy), NSAID gastroprotection in risk patients, Zollinger-Ellison."},{"label":"H2 blockers","desc":"Less effective than PPI — useful in mild GERD or as add-on (nocturnal acid breakthrough)."},{"label":"Antacids","desc":"Symptomatic relief fast but short-lived (1-2 h). No healing of ulcers or esophagitis."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"High-risk GI bleeding: IV PPI (pantoprazole) continuous infusion before endoscopy — reduces actively bleeding Forrest Ia lesions.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"GERD","detail":"Omeprazole/pantoprazole 20-40 mg 1×/day, 30 min before breakfast. 4-8 weeks, then try tapering."},{"naam":"Duodenal ulcer","detail":"PPI + H. pylori eradication (amoxicillin + clarithromycin + PPI 7-14 days). After eradication: PPI 4 weeks."},{"naam":"Long-term use","detail":"Lowest effective dose. Annual re-evaluation whether PPI is still needed."}]},{"_i":246,"q":"52-year-old woman, right upper quadrant pain after fatty meals, nausea. Abdominal ultrasound: gallstones visible, gallbladder normal size. Diagnosis?","a":["Symptomatic cholecystolithiasis","Acute cholecystitis","Choledocholithiasis","Hepatitis A"],"ex":"Symptomatic cholecystolithiasis (biliary colic): right upper quadrant pain after meals, nausea, gallbladder normal size on ultrasound. In acute cholecystitis, the gallbladder is enlarged, wall thickened, with positive Murphy sign.","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"Gallstones present in 10-15% of the Western population. Most asymptomatic.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Murphy sign","desc":"palpation of the right upper abdomen during inspiration → pain due to pressure on inflamed gallbladder."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"Positive in acute cholecystitis."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"Charcot triad (pain + fever + jaundice) = cholangitis due to stone in common bile duct."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Acute cholecystitis","desc":"fever, leukocytosis, thickened gallbladder wall on ultrasound, positive Murphy."},{"label":"Choledocholithiasis","desc":"jaundice, elevated bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase."},{"label":"Hepatitis A","desc":"also right upper quadrant pain but jaundice, elevated ALT/AST."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Cholecystectomy (laparoscopic): the only curative treatment in symptomatic cholecystolithiasis.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Acute cholecystitis","detail":"first antibiotics + analgesia, cholecystectomy within 72 h or after 6 weeks."}]},{"_i":247,"q":"38-year-old man, recurrent episodes of abdominal pain, bloating, alternating diarrhea and constipation, for years. No weight loss, blood in stool negative. Colonoscopy normal. Diagnosis?","a":["Irritable bowel syndrome (IBS)","Ulcerative colitis","Crohn's disease","Colorectal carcinoma"],"ex":"IBS: functional bowel disorder without organic pathology. Rome IV criteria: recurrent abdominal pain ≥1 day/week over the past 3 months, associated with change in defecation and/or stool consistency. Exclusion of organic pathology is essential.","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"IBS affects 10-15% of the population. Subtypes: IBS-D (diarrhea-dominant), IBS-C (constipation-dominant), IBS-M (mixed).","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Visceral hypersensitivity, disturbed gut-brain axis communication, microbiome changes."},{"title":"Possible triggers","desc":"stress, diet (FODMAP), infection (post-infectious IBS)."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"No structural abnormalities on imaging or biopsy."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"IBD (UC/Crohn)","desc":"bloody diarrhea, weight loss, abnormalities on colonoscopy and biopsy, elevated CRP/calprotectin."},{"label":"Alarm signs ("red flags") in IBS","desc":"blood in stool, weight loss, nocturnal symptoms, positive family history of colorectal carcinoma → further investigation."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Lifestyle advice: low-FODMAP diet, stress management.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Pharmacological","detail":"antispasmodics (mebeverine), loperamide in IBS-D, laxatives in IBS-C."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"Psychological therapy effective in severe symptoms."}]},{"_i":248,"q":"50-year-old man, elevated ALT (5×ULN), fatigue, right upper quadrant discomfort. He does not drink alcohol. Ultrasound: increased liver echogenicity. BMI 34, type 2 diabetes. Most likely diagnosis?","a":["Non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD/NASH)","Alcoholic hepatitis","Viral hepatitis B","Primary biliary cholangitis"],"ex":"NAFLD/NASH: liver steatosis in the absence of significant alcohol consumption. Strongly associated with obesity, type 2 diabetes and metabolic syndrome. NASH (non-alcoholic steatohepatitis) can progress to cirrhosis.","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"NAFLD = spectrum of liver diseases: simple steatosis (NAFL) to steatohepatitis (NASH) to cirrhosis. Prevalence 25% in the Western population, increasing due to the obesity epidemic.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Insulin resistance."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"lipid accumulation in hepatocytes."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"oxidative stress."},{"title":"Step 4","desc":"inflammation."},{"title":"Step 5","desc":"fibrosis. Second hit: mitochondrial dysfunction, intestinal dysbiosis, adipokine imbalance."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Alcoholic hepatitis","desc":"alcohol history, AST/ALT ratio >2:1."},{"label":"Viral hepatitis B/C","desc":"serology positive."},{"label":"PBC","desc":"middle-aged women, AMA positive, pruritus, cholestatic pattern."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Weight reduction (10% weight loss significantly improves histology).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 1","detail":"Treat metabolic risk factors (diabetes, hypertension, dyslipidemia)."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"Vitamin E in NASH without diabetes."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"No specific pharmacological therapy proven effective; new agents under investigation."}]},{"_i":249,"q":"Which symptom most distinguishes Crohn's disease from ulcerative colitis?","a":["Perianal fistulas and abscesses","Bloody diarrhea","Abdominal cramps","Elevated CRP during flare"],"ex":"Perianal fistulas are characteristic of Crohn's disease due to the transmural, discontinuous inflammation that can cause fistulas. In ulcerative colitis (mucosal inflammation, colon only), perianal fistulas do not occur. Bloody diarrhea, abdominal cramps and elevated CRP can occur in both.","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"Crohn vs UC: Crohn is transmural (skip lesions, perianal fistulas, can affect ileum and oral cavity). UC is mucosal and continuous, always rectum involved, never perianal fistulas.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Transmural (Crohn)","desc":"All layers of bowel wall affected → fistula formation (perianal, enterovesical), strictures, perforation, abscesses."},{"title":"Mucosal (UC)","desc":"Only mucosa and submucosa affected — continuous from rectum proximally. No skip lesions, no perianal fistulas."},{"title":"Serology","desc":"ASCA (anti-Saccharomyces cerevisiae) positive in Crohn (60%). pANCA positive in UC (65%). Not diagnostic — adjunctive."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Crohn","desc":"Skip lesions, transmural, perianal fistulas, ileal involvement, granulomas on biopsy. ASCA positive."},{"label":"Ulcerative colitis","desc":"Continuous rectum → proximal, colon only, mucosal, no fistulas, PSC as extraintestinal manifestation. pANCA positive."},{"label":"Infectious colitis","desc":"Acute onset, fever, positive culture (Campylobacter, Salmonella, C. difficile). Biopsy: no chronic changes."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Severe flare: prednisolone 40 mg/day orally or IV methylprednisolone. Biologics if steroid-refractory.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Mild Crohn","detail":"Budesonide (ileocolic Crohn). Azathioprine/6-MP as maintenance."},{"naam":"Moderate-severe Crohn","detail":"Anti-TNF (infliximab, adalimumab) or vedolizumab/ustekinumab if failure."},{"naam":"UC","detail":"Mesalazine (5-ASA) oral + rectal in mild UC. Severe flare: IV steroids → biologics."}]},{"_i":250,"q":"35-year-old woman, heartburn after meals, regurgitation, nocturnal cough. Symptoms improve with head elevation. Which diagnosis is most likely?","a":["Gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD)","Gastric ulcer","Achalasia","Eosinophilic esophagitis"],"ex":"GERD: classic heartburn + regurgitation, worsening when lying down or after meals, improvement with antacids/PPI and head elevation. Nocturnal cough and hoarseness are atypical manifestations of GERD due to laryngeal irritation.","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"GERD: gastric contents flow back into the esophagus due to insufficient lower esophageal sphincter. Prevalence 10-20% in the Western world.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Barrett esophagus","desc":"metaplasia of squamous epithelium to columnar intestinal epithelium due to chronic exposure to gastric acid."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"Risk factor for esophageal adenocarcinoma (30-40× increased risk)."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"Surveillance endoscopy every 3-5 years."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Achalasia","desc":"dysphagia for solids and liquids, non-acid regurgitation, manometry: increased LES pressure."},{"label":"Eosinophilic esophagitis","desc":"dysphagia in young people, bolus impaction, eosinophilia on biopsy."},{"label":"Gastric ulcer","desc":"epigastric pain, meal-dependent."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Lifestyle: weight reduction, sleep with head elevated, avoid triggers (alcohol, coffee, chocolate, fat).","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 1","detail":"PPI 4-8 weeks."},{"naam":"Step 2","detail":"Maintenance therapy with recurrence."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"Fundoplication (surgery) in treatment resistance."}]},{"_i":251,"q":"Helicobacter pylori infection is a risk factor for both gastric ulcer and gastric carcinoma.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. H. pylori is present in >90% of duodenal ulcers and ~70% of gastric ulcers. It is also classified as an IARC group 1 carcinogen for gastric carcinoma. Eradication reduces ulcer recurrence and carcinoma risk.","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"H. pylori colonizes the gastric mucosa and causes chronic gastritis. Risk factor for duodenal ulcer (>90%), gastric ulcer (~70%), MALT lymphoma and gastric carcinoma. Eradication reduces ulcer recurrence and carcinoma risk.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Virulence factors","desc":"CagA (oncoprotein) and VacA (vacuolating cytotoxin) → mucosal inflammation → atrophic gastritis → intestinal metaplasia → gastric carcinoma."},{"title":"Ulcer mechanism","desc":"H. pylori reduces mucosal defense and increases gastrin production → acid hypersecretion → ulcer."},{"title":"MALT lymphoma","desc":"Mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue lymphoma — 70-80% cure after H. pylori eradication alone."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Urea breath test (UBT)","desc":"Gold standard for non-invasive diagnosis and verification of eradication. Stop PPI 2 weeks before test."},{"label":"Stool antigen","desc":"Alternative to UBT — stop PPI 2 weeks. Cheap and reliable."},{"label":"Biopsy (CLO test/histology)","desc":"With gastroscopy — directly during endoscopy. Needed with alarm symptoms or treatment failure."},{"label":"Serology","desc":"Proves exposure, not active infection — not suitable for eradication control."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Triple therapy 7-14 days: PPI + amoxicillin + clarithromycin. Or quadruple therapy with resistance.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Triple therapy","detail":"PPI 2×/day + amoxicillin 1 g 2×/day + clarithromycin 500 mg 2×/day × 7-14 days."},{"naam":"With penicillin allergy","detail":"PPI + metronidazole + clarithromycin (or bismuth quadruple therapy)."},{"naam":"Eradication control","detail":"Urea breath test 4-6 weeks after end of antibiotics (PPI stopped 2 weeks). Eradication in >95% with adequate therapy."}]},{"_i":252,"q":"With suspected upper GI bleeding, an abdominal CT scan must always be performed before endoscopy.","a":null,"ex":"FALSE. Urgent endoscopy (gastroscopy) within 24 hours is the first choice in upper GI bleeding. Endoscopy is both diagnostic and therapeutic (hemostasis possible). CT angiography is considered only if endoscopy fails or if the source remains unclear.","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"Upper GI bleeding: resuscitate first, then endoscopy within 24 h (or <12 h with hemodynamic instability). CT scan does not have diagnostic priority over endoscopy — endoscopy is also therapeutic.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Forrest classification","desc":"Ia: active spurting (immediate hemostasis). IIa/IIb: visible vessel/clot (intervention). IIc/III: hematin/clean base (conservative)."},{"title":"Pre-endoscopic PPI","desc":"IV pantoprazole before endoscopy → reduces high-risk stigmata → fewer interventions needed."},{"title":"Hemostasis methods","desc":"Epinephrine injection, hemostatic clips, thermal coagulation, argon plasma coagulation."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Gastroscopy (first choice)","desc":"Diagnostic and therapeutic. Within 24 h (stable) or <12 h (unstable). Forrest classification determines management."},{"label":"CT angiography","desc":"With massive bleeding or if source unclear after endoscopy. Can prepare embolization."},{"label":"Colonoscopy","desc":"In lower GI bleeding (bright red blood per anus) — after stabilization and preparation."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Resuscitation: IV access ×2, crystalloids, blood products if Hb <8 g/dL. IV PPI immediately.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Pre-endoscopy","detail":"Pantoprazole 80 mg IV bolus → 8 mg/h continuous. Consider IV erythromycin as prokinetic (empties stomach)."},{"naam":"Endoscopic hemostasis","detail":"Forrest Ia/IIa: clip + injection. Success >90%. Recurrence: repeat endoscopy or angiography."},{"naam":"After hemostasis","detail":"Oral PPI 2×/day 72 h, then daily. Test and eradicate H. pylori if present."}]},{"_i":253,"q":"An acute abdomen with pneumoperitoneum on chest X-ray is an absolute indication for emergency laparotomy.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Free air under the diaphragm (pneumoperitoneum) indicates a perforated hollow organ (stomach, duodenum, colon). This is a surgical emergency. The most frequent cause is perforation of a gastric or duodenal ulcer.","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"Pneumoperitoneum = free air under the diaphragm on upright chest X-ray → perforated hollow organ until proven otherwise. Surgical emergency. Most frequent cause: gastric or duodenal ulcer perforation.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Perforation cascade","desc":"Perforation → air + gastric/intestinal contents leak into abdominal cavity → chemical peritonitis → bacterial peritonitis → sepsis."},{"title":"Clinical picture","desc":"Rigid abdomen ("board-like abdomen"), diffuse tenderness, rebound tenderness, tachycardia. Patient lies still (movement painful)."},{"title":"CT abdomen","desc":"More sensitive than chest X-ray for small amounts of free gas — always if in doubt. Also shows the source."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"True perforation","desc":"Sudden abdominal pain, board-like abdomen, fever, pneumoperitoneum on chest X-ray/CT. Surgical emergency."},{"label":"Pseudopneumoperitoneum","desc":"Chilaiditi syndrome (colon between liver and diaphragm) — no symptoms, no surgery needed. Clinical correlation essential."},{"label":"Ischemic colitis","desc":"Abdominal pain + hematochezia but no pneumoperitoneum initially. CT: colonic wall thickening."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Emergency laparotomy or laparoscopy — no delay. Nothing by mouth.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Preoperative","detail":"IV access, fluid resuscitation, antibiotics (piperacillin/tazobactam or cefuroxime + metronidazole), nasogastric tube."},{"naam":"Surgery","detail":"Gastric perforation: primary closure + omental patch (Graham technique). Colon perforation: Hartmann procedure or primary anastomosis."},{"naam":"Postoperative","detail":"ICU monitoring, PPI, H. pylori eradication if ulcer was the cause."}]},{"_i":254,"q":"Colonoscopy is the first-choice screening method for colorectal carcinoma in a 50-year-old without special risk factors.","a":null,"ex":"TRUE. Colonoscopy is the gold standard for colorectal carcinoma screening: detection and immediate removal of polyps. In the Netherlands, however, fecal immunochemical testing (FIT) is used as population screening, with colonoscopy after positive FIT.","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"Colorectal carcinoma: 3rd most common cancer, 2nd cause of cancer death. Adenoma-carcinoma sequence takes 10-15 years — the window for successful screening and prevention. Colonoscopy is gold standard: diagnostic and therapeutic.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Adenoma-carcinoma sequence","desc":"Adenoma → APC mutation → K-ras mutation → p53 loss → invasive carcinoma. 10-15 year time window for preventive polypectomy."},{"title":"Risk factors","desc":"Age >50 years, IBD, family history CRC, FAP (APC mutation), Lynch syndrome (HNPCC, MMR genes)."},{"title":"CEA","desc":"Not suitable for screening — too nonspecific. Useful for recurrence monitoring after curative resection."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Colonoscopy","desc":"Gold standard: diagnostic + therapeutic (polypectomy). Once every 10 years with normal result."},{"label":"FIT (fecal immunochemical testing)","desc":"Dutch population screening: non-invasive, annual or biennial. Positive → colonoscopy."},{"label":"CT colonography","desc":"Non-invasive alternative but no therapeutic option (polyp removal impossible)."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Obstruction due to CRC: urgent colonoscopy or CT colonography → stent placement or Hartmann procedure.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Stage I-II","detail":"Surgical resection curative. Adjuvant chemotherapy in stage III (FOLFOX)."},{"naam":"Stage IV (metastases)","detail":"Chemotherapy (FOLFOX or FOLFIRI) ± bevacizumab/cetuximab depending on RAS mutation status."},{"naam":"Lynch syndrome","detail":"Genetic testing of family members. More intensive surveillance. Immunotherapy (pembrolizumab) effective in MSI-H CRC."}]},{"_i":255,"q":"70-year-old patient with suspected upper GI bleeding (hematemesis, hemodynamically stable). Which investigation has the highest priority?","a":["Gastroscopy within 24 hours","CT abdomen with contrast","Arterial angiography","Upright abdominal X-ray"],"ex":"Gastroscopy is both diagnostic and therapeutic (hemostasis). In stable patient: within 24 h. In hemodynamically unstable patient: resuscitate first, then gastroscopy as soon as possible (<12 h). CT has no therapeutic benefit initially.","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"Gastroscopy is the first step in upper GI bleeding — diagnostic and therapeutic. Glasgow-Blatchford Score determines urgency. Pre-endoscopic IV PPI reduces high-risk stigmata.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Glasgow-Blatchford Score","desc":"Score 0: safely outpatient. High score (>6): urgent endoscopy. Determined based on Hb, urea, systolic BP, heart rate, comorbidity."},{"title":"Endoscopic hemostasis","desc":"Epinephrine injection, hemostatic clips, thermal coagulation, APC. Success >90% in active bleeding."},{"title":"Recurrent bleeding","desc":"Repeat endoscopy → if failure: angiography with embolization → surgery as last resort."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Gastroscopy (first choice)","desc":"Diagnostic + therapeutic. Stable: within 24 h. Unstable: after resuscitation <12 h."},{"label":"Angiography/embolization","desc":"With recurrence after endoscopy or if source endoscopically inaccessible."},{"label":"Upright abdominal X-ray","desc":"No role in GI bleeding — useful in suspected perforation (free gas under diaphragm)."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"IV pantoprazole 80 mg bolus → 8 mg/h continuous. Gastroscopy as soon as possible.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Blood transfusion","detail":"Restrictive policy: transfusion with Hb <8 g/dL (cirrhosis patients: <7 g/dL — over-transfusion increases portal pressure)."},{"naam":"Variceal bleeding","detail":"Terlipressin + antibiotics (norfloxacin) + endoscopic ligation — treat variceal bleeding differently from peptic ulcer bleeding immediately."},{"naam":"After hemostasis","detail":"Test/eradicate H. pylori. Stop NSAID. Maintenance PPI in risk patients."}]},{"_i":256,"q":"68-year-old man, acute severe abdominal pain, fever 39°C, left lower quadrant pain, left-sided guarding. CT abdomen: thickened sigmoid colon, inflamed pericolic fat. Diagnosis?","a":["Acute diverticulitis","Sigmoid colon carcinoma","Ischemic colitis","Incarcerated inguinal hernia"],"ex":"Acute diverticulitis: acute inflammation of a diverticulum (outpouching of the colon wall). Classic: left lower quadrant pain (sigmoid colon most affected), fever, leukocytosis. CT abdomen is diagnostic: pericolic fat, thickened colon wall.","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"Diverticulosis (presence of diverticula) in >50% of the Western population >70 years. Complications: diverticulitis (20%), bleeding, fistula, abscess, perforation.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Diverticulitis occurs with obstruction of a diverticular neck."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"bacterial proliferation."},{"title":"Step 3","desc":"micro- or macroperforation. Hinchey severity classification (I-IV): I = pericolic abscess, IV = fecal peritonitis."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Colon carcinoma","desc":"slower course, weight loss, changed bowel habit, blood loss."},{"label":"Ischemic colitis","desc":"acute, left-sided, hematochezia, older, vascular risk factors."},{"label":"Appendicitis","desc":"right lower quadrant, McBurney."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Uncomplicated: antibiotics (ciprofloxacin + metronidazole or amoxicillin-clavulanate), bowel rest diet.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Complicated (abscess)","detail":"CT-guided drainage."},{"naam":"Perforation","detail":"emergency laparotomy (Hartmann procedure)."},{"naam":"Step 3","detail":"Elective sigmoid resection after ≥2 complicated episodes."}]},{"_i":257,"q":"42-year-old woman with liver cirrhosis (Child-Pugh B). Acute hematemesis, blood pressure 90/60 mmHg. Most likely bleeding source?","a":["Esophageal varices","Gastric ulcer","Mallory-Weiss tear","Angiodysplasia"],"ex":"In a cirrhosis patient with hematemesis, esophageal varices are the most likely cause (portal hypertension → varices). Mortality per bleeding episode 15-25%. Immediate treatment: terlipressin + somatostatin analogue + antibiotics + endoscopy.","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"Portal hypertension (portal pressure >10 mmHg) in cirrhosis → formation of varices in esophagus and gastric fundus → risk of rupture. Variceal bleeding occurs in 25-35% of cirrhosis patients.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Acute management","desc":"resuscitation (cautious with fluids!), terlipressin (or somatostatin/octreotide) IV reduces portal pressure, antibiotics (norfloxacin → infection prophylaxis), endoscopy with ligation or sclerotherapy."},{"title":"Step 2","desc":"TIPS in refractory bleeding."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Gastric ulcer","desc":"also in cirrhosis but less acute shock."},{"label":"Mallory-Weiss","desc":"tear at gastroesophageal junction after vomiting, less severe."},{"label":"Portal gastropathy","desc":"diffuse mucosal bleeding, also in cirrhosis."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Balloon tamponade (Sengstaken-Blakemore): temporary in massive bleeding.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 1","detail":"TIPS (transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt): refractory variceal bleeding."},{"naam":"Secondary prophylaxis","detail":"beta-blocker + endoscopic variceal ligation."}]},{"_i":258,"q":"25-year-old man, progressive dysphagia for solids and liquids, regurgitation of undigested food. Manometry: increased lower esophageal sphincter pressure, aperistalsis. Diagnosis?","a":["Achalasia","Gastroesophageal reflux","Esophageal carcinoma","Eosinophilic esophagitis"],"ex":"Achalasia: neuromuscular disorder of the esophagus with loss of nitrergic inhibitory neurons → incomplete relaxation of the lower esophageal sphincter + aperistalsis. Dysphagia for both solids and liquids is characteristic (distinguishes from carcinoma).","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"Achalasia: absence of inhibitory nitrergic myenteric plexus neurons → LES relaxes insufficiently with swallowing → esophageal retention → regurgitation. Barium swallow: "bird-beak" narrowing.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Step 1","desc":"Cause unknown (idiopathic); secondary achalasia: Chagas disease (Trypanosoma cruzi), pseudoachalasia due to tumor at cardia."},{"title":"Manometry is gold standard","desc":"increased basal LES pressure and incomplete LES relaxation."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Esophageal carcinoma","desc":"older, weight loss, smoking/alcohol, progressive dysphagia more for solids than liquids."},{"label":"GERD","desc":"heartburn, no dysphagia as main complaint."},{"label":"Eosinophilic esophagitis","desc":"young people, atopy, food allergy, bolus impaction."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Endoscopic pneumatic dilation of the LES.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Step 1","detail":"Laparoscopic Heller myotomy (+ Dor fundoplication)."},{"naam":"POEM (Per Oral Endoscopic Myotomy)","detail":"minimally invasive."},{"naam":"Botox injection","detail":"temporary, for patients who are not operable."}]},{"_i":259,"q":"What is the most common cause of liver cirrhosis in the Western world?","a":["Alcoholic liver disease","Viral hepatitis C","NAFLD/NASH","Primary biliary cholangitis"],"ex":"Alcoholic liver disease is the most common cause of cirrhosis in Western countries, followed by viral hepatitis C and NAFLD/NASH (increasing due to obesity epidemic). Worldwide, viral hepatitis B is the most frequent cause.","dl":"Gastroenterology","wiki_kern":"Alcoholic liver disease is the most common cause of cirrhosis in Western countries. Cirrhosis = end stage: fibrosis + portal hypertension → ascites, varices, encephalopathy, SBP, HCC.","wiki_redflag":null,"wiki_bigfact_label":null,"wiki_bigfact_sub":null,"wiki_mechanisme":[{"title":"Alcoholic hepatitis","desc":"Direct hepatotoxicity + acetaldehyde damage + oxidative stress → steatohepatitis → fibrosis → cirrhosis."},{"title":"Child-Pugh score","desc":"A/B/C based on bilirubin, albumin, prothrombin time, ascites, encephalopathy. Child C = poor prognosis."},{"title":"MELD score","desc":"Determines priority on liver transplant list (creatinine, bilirubin, INR). Score >15 = consider transplantation."}],"wiki_onderscheid":[{"label":"Alcoholic cirrhosis","desc":"Alcohol history, AST/ALT >2:1 with active drinking, MCV high. Abstinence slows progression."},{"label":"HCV cirrhosis","desc":"Anti-HCV positive, HCV PCR positive. Curative DAA therapy (>95% SVR). Cirrhosis regression possible."},{"label":"NAFLD/NASH","desc":"Increasing due to obesity epidemic. No alcohol. Metabolic syndrome. Weight reduction as therapy."},{"label":"Hemochromatosis","desc":"High ferritin + transferrin saturation >45%. Genetic (HFE mutation). Phlebotomy as therapy."}],"wiki_therapie_urgent":"Hepatic encephalopathy: lactulose 3×/day, rifaximin. Ascites: spironolactone ± furosemide. Variceal bleeding: terlipressin + endoscopy.","wiki_therapie_stappen":[{"naam":"Alcohol abstinence","detail":"Only intervention that slows progression and can sometimes induce cirrhosis regression. Multidisciplinary support."},{"naam":"SBP prophylaxis","detail":"Norfloxacin or ciprofloxacin orally with ascites TP <15 g/L or after first SBP episode."},{"naam":"HCC screening","detail":"Liver ultrasound + AFP every 6 months — cirrhosis patients have increased HCC risk."}]}]}
